Yes.

242
00:13:15,645 --> 00:13:16,846
So we humanized Him.

243
00:13:17,184 --> 00:13:19,705
We'll be back to IGDLCC after I
tell you a few things about

244
00:13:19,745 --> 00:13:21,225
our partners at GoodWe.

245
00:13:21,385 --> 00:13:25,823
For three months now, at Cavaleria Hub,
we've had their ESA All-in-One installed.

246
00:13:26,145 --> 00:13:28,544
I'm not telling you about it
like in a product presentation.

247
00:13:28,586 --> 00:13:32,005
I'm telling you what I've seen firsthand
after three months of real-world use.

248
00:13:32,405 --> 00:13:36,325
You know that here at the Hub we've built
one of the most sophisticated

249
00:13:36,365 --> 00:13:38,184
storage systems we've ever made.

250
00:13:38,485 --> 00:13:41,505
Precisely because we started from
a poor grid connection and were forced

251
00:13:41,544 --> 00:13:43,245
to make do with little power.

252
00:13:43,466 --> 00:13:46,966
ESA All-in-One came in exactly
where we needed to expand.

253
00:13:47,245 --> 00:13:47,944
What's different about it?

254
00:13:48,285 --> 00:13:50,944
The inverter, the battery and all
the smarts for managing

255
00:13:51,005 --> 00:13:52,565
energy sit in a single system.

256
00:13:52,905 --> 00:13:55,085
You don't cobble together an inverter
with a battery from another

257
00:13:55,105 --> 00:13:56,804
manufacturer and hope it works.

258
00:13:57,025 --> 00:14:00,145
It comes fully integrated, ready to
automate on its own when you produce,

259
00:14:00,184 --> 00:14:01,645
when you store, and when you consume.

260
00:14:02,125 --> 00:14:03,765
What I liked most
in these three months?

261
00:14:04,086 --> 00:14:04,846
I don't think about it anymore.

262
00:14:05,044 --> 00:14:07,966
It ran automatically, and I almost
forgot it has an app, because

263
00:14:08,005 --> 00:14:09,365
we haven't had a single fault so far.

264
00:14:09,424 --> 00:14:13,044
And if you already have solar without
storage, you don't have to start over.

265
00:14:13,424 --> 00:14:16,085
ESA All-in-One also integrates
with an existing system.

266
00:14:16,424 --> 00:14:19,424
You add storage and automation
without tearing down what you have.

267
00:14:19,826 --> 00:14:23,105
It's not just any battery, it's a system
designed for someone who wants real

268
00:14:23,285 --> 00:14:26,585
control over their energy, not just
a box that holds backup power.

269
00:14:27,086 --> 00:14:30,725
If you want to learn more,
there's a link in the description.

270
00:14:31,125 --> 00:14:34,885
And if you'd like to work with us too,
write to us at contact@buhnici.ro.

271
00:14:35,385 --> 00:14:37,125
And now, back to the conversation.

272
00:14:37,306 --> 00:14:42,846
There's a constant phenomenon in the
history of human culture and civilization

273
00:14:44,009 --> 00:14:46,230
which is called anthropomorphism.

274
00:14:46,509 --> 00:14:49,169
Anthropos: man. Morphe: form.

275
00:14:49,909 --> 00:14:57,009
Giving human form to natural
or supernatural phenomena.

276
00:14:57,409 --> 00:15:01,629
So it's not a mistake
our contemporaries make.

277
00:15:02,169 --> 00:15:08,289
So we have this tendency
to give a name and a face to

278
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:10,169
things that can't have them.

279
00:15:10,669 --> 00:15:15,209
That's how primitive religions were born,
how idolatry was born, how there came

280
00:15:15,289 --> 00:15:18,289
the idols, the gods of Olympus, all that.

281
00:15:18,830 --> 00:15:26,470
That's how Disney made money,
an immense fortune, by

282
00:15:26,490 --> 00:15:31,210
anthropomorphizing animals and making
them so likable and so on that

283
00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:33,610
the whole world watches them avidly.

284
00:15:33,970 --> 00:15:34,350
Why?

285
00:15:34,830 --> 00:15:38,750
Because we recognize our own
reactions, attitudes,

286
00:15:38,809 --> 00:15:43,489
behaviors in the reactions,
attitudes and behaviors

287
00:15:43,549 --> 00:15:46,069
of the animals in cartoons.

288
00:15:46,590 --> 00:15:50,910
Well, in what you're saying
there's a form of anthropomorphization.

289
00:15:51,009 --> 00:15:56,210
We're sure there's someone
out there, and we're sure that Fritz

290
00:15:56,389 --> 00:16:03,870
is the Germans' man, Bolojan is
the French people's man, or

291
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:12,710
Nicușor Dan is Macron's man, and
Bolojan takes orders from Brussels,

292
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:20,050
and there's always a puppet master, and
different groups with different readings

293
00:16:20,649 --> 00:16:23,250
of reality find a different puppet master.

294
00:16:23,809 --> 00:16:27,689
And then we no longer know why...

295
00:16:30,354 --> 00:16:33,714
Events aren't coherent
and we don't know what to believe,

296
00:16:34,273 --> 00:16:35,574
and they catch us by surprise.

297
00:16:36,313 --> 00:16:43,813
Especially us Romanians, we have this
need for that guy to be with those guys.

298
00:16:45,514 --> 00:16:46,533
Yes, he belongs to someone.

299
00:16:47,293 --> 00:16:48,394
And who do you belong to?

300
00:16:50,394 --> 00:16:50,573
To...

301
00:16:52,894 --> 00:17:00,254
I'm a child of God, and otherwise I'm
my children's father and my wife's husband.

302
00:17:02,214 --> 00:17:06,673
So I've never belonged to... You might
also be a child of your own ideas.

303
00:17:07,453 --> 00:17:13,053
Maybe, but I also refused to serve in
the Securitate, they wanted to take me.

304
00:17:13,314 --> 00:17:15,954
They summoned me to enlist several times.

305
00:17:16,814 --> 00:17:21,054
I was supposed to serve at the school
in Grădiște, near Bucharest.

306
00:17:21,534 --> 00:17:27,014
I didn't want to, because I knew it meant
belonging, probably for

307
00:17:27,034 --> 00:17:31,274
the rest of your life, to shadowy structures.

308
00:17:32,213 --> 00:17:35,453
Yes, but lately I've seen you with
the Romanian Academy more often.

309
00:17:36,054 --> 00:17:39,494
Are you heading toward that
ivory tower, elite zone?

310
00:17:40,433 --> 00:17:41,634
No, not necessarily, no.

311
00:17:42,753 --> 00:17:42,873
No.

312
00:17:43,193 --> 00:17:45,533
Well, maybe you'll teach them to
talk, to come down from there.

313
00:17:45,653 --> 00:17:46,294
Now that, yes.

314
00:17:46,594 --> 00:17:48,173
They should come out into the public arena.

315
00:17:48,193 --> 00:17:48,733
You brought them here.

316
00:17:49,074 --> 00:17:50,413
We talked about demography at one point.

317
00:17:50,513 --> 00:17:51,053
Very good.

318
00:17:51,354 --> 00:17:52,314
That was a very good discussion.

319
00:17:52,614 --> 00:17:53,953
Yes, did it get an audience?

320
00:17:54,773 --> 00:17:55,253
Decent.

321
00:17:57,094 --> 00:17:57,814
Did you see any echoes?

322
00:17:58,913 --> 00:17:59,074
No.

323
00:17:59,634 --> 00:18:00,774
Then what does the audience matter?

324
00:18:04,397 --> 00:18:05,317
Yes, you're right.

325
00:18:05,978 --> 00:18:13,097
So I would drop the idea of finding
who's behind someone, because

326
00:18:13,157 --> 00:18:18,798
that's a form of mysticism and primitive
thinking, symbolic thinking.

327
00:18:19,157 --> 00:18:20,237
Well, then how do we do it, Professor?

328
00:18:21,077 --> 00:18:27,397
Well, we have to go for rationalizing
institutions, rationalizing

329
00:18:28,057 --> 00:18:32,458
communication, and pressing as hard
as we can on those

330
00:18:32,498 --> 00:18:34,538
who could change something.

331
00:18:34,978 --> 00:18:38,678
And when we have a choice, we should
choose those who want change, not

332
00:18:38,758 --> 00:18:40,057
those who want to preserve things.

333
00:18:40,798 --> 00:18:41,837
Thank you for saying that.

334
00:18:42,258 --> 00:18:43,798
Then let me raise the next question.

335
00:18:44,117 --> 00:18:46,177
You didn't hold back, you were
among the first

336
00:18:46,178 --> 00:18:47,458
to say Nicușor Dan had betrayed us.

337
00:18:47,857 --> 00:18:47,957
Yes.

338
00:18:48,417 --> 00:18:53,057
At the time it seemed like a statement
that was a bit too strong, a bit

339
00:18:53,137 --> 00:18:55,937
aggressive, perceived as a bit aggressive.

340
00:18:56,978 --> 00:19:00,097
What happened afterward not only
confirmed, it reinforces what

341
00:19:00,157 --> 00:19:03,577
you're saying, because we
voted for reform through Nicușor Dan

342
00:19:04,798 --> 00:19:06,978
As part of this liberal pole, right?

343
00:19:07,938 --> 00:19:11,498
Let's put it this way, a pole, USR
together with PNL, that's roughly it.

344
00:19:12,438 --> 00:19:13,478
And he promised us reform.

345
00:19:13,538 --> 00:19:14,557
Not only did he not deliver reform.

346
00:19:15,018 --> 00:19:17,837
After taking office, the first thing
he did was start visiting monasteries.

347
00:19:18,218 --> 00:19:19,918
He gives speeches to Orthodox youth.

348
00:19:20,458 --> 00:19:25,798
He talks about sovereignism...
Healthy nationalism.

349
00:19:25,958 --> 00:19:26,138
Right.

350
00:19:27,357 --> 00:19:28,437
Whatever that is, I don't know.

351
00:19:28,538 --> 00:19:30,238
Maybe he'll explain it
to us at some point.

352
00:19:30,817 --> 00:19:33,177
He promised us the report on the elections.

353
00:19:33,998 --> 00:19:38,018
He promised us new heads
of the intelligence services.

354
00:19:39,218 --> 00:19:43,178
He promised us what else... too many,
far too many things.

355
00:19:43,218 --> 00:19:47,538
And now, for example, he refuses to
call early elections, although it's clear

356
00:19:47,938 --> 00:19:51,938
the parties currently
in the coalition can't coordinate.

357
00:19:52,057 --> 00:19:57,218
The people you were talking about,
they can't manage to be consistent.

358
00:19:57,597 --> 00:20:00,457
At least in the '90s those guys knew
they wanted us to join the European Union.

359
00:20:00,698 --> 00:20:11,138
Yes, that saved the political class from
this disaster of non-communication.

360
00:20:12,577 --> 00:20:18,917
All of that was called the European acquis,
meaning a list of tasks we had

361
00:20:18,978 --> 00:20:26,577
to... Yes ...to fulfill in order to...
27,000 normative acts, regulations

362
00:20:26,798 --> 00:20:30,018
that had to be implemented in local
legislation, the famous EU acquis.

363
00:20:30,678 --> 00:20:33,118
In the end they gave us some derogations.

364
00:20:33,178 --> 00:20:38,318
They said: public administration, you can
do it after joining the European

365
00:20:38,397 --> 00:20:45,117
Union; the judiciary, you can
reform it afterward, but we'll create a

366
00:20:45,157 --> 00:20:53,877
mechanism for cooperation and... And
verification ...verification, the CVM, which

367
00:20:53,917 --> 00:20:58,077
will apply to both Romania and Bulgaria,
to help you reform the judiciary.

368
00:20:58,597 --> 00:21:01,917
Unfortunately, it was abolished,
and you've seen where that led.

369
00:21:02,637 --> 00:21:12,377
To a justice system shaped by
a patri... a parochial type of culture.

370
00:21:12,877 --> 00:21:18,577
It's no longer a culture of... I was going
to say patriarchal, but it's matriarchal.

371
00:21:18,698 --> 00:21:23,317
No, yes, Mrs. Lia is a mother, not a father.

372
00:21:24,278 --> 00:21:34,001
So... We're in a period where we're
finding out Nicușor didn't actually betray.

373
00:21:34,061 --> 00:21:34,701
Do you know what he did?

374
00:21:34,741 --> 00:21:35,341
He lied.

375
00:21:36,642 --> 00:21:43,701
He wasn't open to change
in the authentic sense, toward Europe.

376
00:21:44,082 --> 00:21:48,182
And you know, in that famous love
letter he sent to Dilema,

377
00:21:48,701 --> 00:21:53,201
he says this: "I lived six years in
the West and I didn't like it. I didn't

378
00:21:53,241 --> 00:21:58,581
like it because it encourages individual
identity at the expense of group

379
00:21:58,622 --> 00:22:06,582
identity," that is, that "moi numéro," as
the French say, herd identity, but which

380
00:22:06,662 --> 00:22:09,701
he calls community identity.

381
00:22:10,541 --> 00:22:15,841
He says: "I feel the community
flowing through all the veins of my

382
00:22:15,882 --> 00:22:20,902
being, with all my being. I'm
for community." Which one?

383
00:22:21,521 --> 00:22:26,942
Well, a community can be a lifeline
that helps you out of an impasse,

384
00:22:27,541 --> 00:22:28,961
but it can also be diabolical.

385
00:22:29,842 --> 00:22:31,240
There are diabolical communities.

386
00:22:31,881 --> 00:22:38,822
I don't know if you've seen
that Von Trier film, Dogville.

387
00:22:39,162 --> 00:22:39,741
Have you seen it?

388
00:22:40,241 --> 00:22:42,241
With Nicole Kidman in the lead role.

389
00:22:42,882 --> 00:22:47,582
A mobster's daughter who runs away
from home because she couldn't stand

390
00:22:47,622 --> 00:22:49,201
the mobsters' way of life.

391
00:22:49,442 --> 00:22:49,701
Right.

392
00:22:49,942 --> 00:22:55,122
A teenager runs away from home, ends up in
the Rocky Mountains, in an isolated village.

393
00:22:55,541 --> 00:22:57,981
That's Dogville, the town of dogs.

394
00:22:58,442 --> 00:23:05,541
And there, those people do everything
you can do to a young, beautiful woman.

395
00:23:06,021 --> 00:23:07,041
Everything you can do.

396
00:23:07,741 --> 00:23:12,882
They didn't miss a thing, and there's always
a tacit understanding and complicity.

397
00:23:13,481 --> 00:23:19,190
They communicated with looks. To her face
they said one thing, but did another.

398
00:23:19,769 --> 00:23:25,990
That's the diabolical community that I
loathe, and that we've seen in

399
00:23:26,710 --> 00:23:32,710
the various forms of fascism, yes,
in Europe and in other parts of the world.

400
00:23:33,089 --> 00:23:37,129
So community shouldn't be idolized,
because it can be both good and bad.

401
00:23:37,809 --> 00:23:43,909
Mr. Nicușor Dan idolizes it
and says he is for a

402
00:23:44,750 --> 00:23:48,829
healthy nationalism, which he even
defines in that letter

403
00:23:49,450 --> 00:23:54,730
as a nationalism that doesn't lead
to chauvinism, racism and xenophobia.

404
00:23:55,430 --> 00:23:59,410
But a nationalism that doesn't lead
to that is hard to conceive.

405
00:24:00,089 --> 00:24:02,230
That's the very definition of nationalism.

406
00:24:02,869 --> 00:24:07,509
It's an ethnocentrism that makes you
despise... The other.

407
00:24:07,549 --> 00:24:09,450
The other, who is different from you.

408
00:24:10,009 --> 00:24:13,769
So... And what does the other look like,
the one not good enough for your list?

409
00:24:14,029 --> 00:24:15,430
He's simply different.

410
00:24:15,569 --> 00:24:22,710
He might have democratic ideals,
he might want the rule

411
00:24:22,769 --> 00:24:24,629
of law, equality before the law.

412
00:24:25,029 --> 00:24:29,609
He might want an administration
without corruption.

413
00:24:30,049 --> 00:24:31,069
He's different from you.

414
00:24:31,109 --> 00:24:36,289
You don't hate him the way
PSD people hate the USR people.

415
00:24:36,430 --> 00:24:37,310
I don't know if you've noticed.

416
00:24:37,390 --> 00:24:38,150
Oh yes, it's hatred.

417
00:24:38,210 --> 00:24:38,710
It's hatred.

418
00:24:38,769 --> 00:24:39,569
It's class hatred there.

419
00:24:39,630 --> 00:24:40,470
It's class hatred.

420
00:24:40,970 --> 00:24:43,829
Actually, these people don't fit their ethos.

421
00:24:44,450 --> 00:24:49,370
They can't be manipulated, they can't be
saddled, you can't give them orders,

422
00:24:49,430 --> 00:24:53,930
because they have other values, like
human dignity, the moral autonomy of

423
00:24:53,970 --> 00:24:57,589
the person, respect for the rules.

424
00:24:57,910 --> 00:24:58,190
Yes.

425
00:24:58,230 --> 00:24:59,150
All these things.

426
00:24:59,630 --> 00:25:02,390
But do you know what I want
to tell you, Mr. Buhnici?

427
00:25:02,809 --> 00:25:04,710
Not respect for all the rules.

428
00:25:05,630 --> 00:25:05,870
Yes.

429
00:25:05,910 --> 00:25:06,970
For their own rules.

430
00:25:07,670 --> 00:25:08,509
Yes, well.

431
00:25:09,170 --> 00:25:14,050
What I want to tell you is that everything
you presented at the beginning

432
00:25:14,089 --> 00:25:18,689
of our conversation are events

433
00:25:21,013 --> 00:25:27,593
that are real, undeniable, harmful and
serious, but they're only consequences.

434
00:25:28,693 --> 00:25:33,693
They're second-hand, third-hand
or tenth-hand consequences of

435
00:25:34,794 --> 00:25:39,854
much deeper struggles,
invisible to the naked eye.

436
00:25:40,153 --> 00:25:40,953
What are those struggles?

437
00:25:41,253 --> 00:25:46,613
You know how it is, I always keep in mind
that others are watching us too.

438
00:25:47,134 --> 00:25:52,054
If it were just the two of us, I wouldn't say
many things, because I know you know them.

439
00:25:52,354 --> 00:25:55,193
We've talked here many times before,
and I encourage those watching,

440
00:25:55,253 --> 00:25:57,153
if they have time and patience, to go through them.

441
00:25:57,233 --> 00:26:01,114
There are people, you know, from all walks
of life who tell me, including people

442
00:26:01,153 --> 00:26:05,354
with a lot of schooling, that they listen
to you even when you explain so simply

443
00:26:05,413 --> 00:26:09,733
complicated things, because we all
have to want it, and I think we need

444
00:26:09,773 --> 00:26:10,933
to go back to school a bit.

445
00:26:11,413 --> 00:26:16,074
Well, I want to explain it like this:
there are several layers of earth.

446
00:26:16,713 --> 00:26:22,354
There's a visible layer, that arable
soil you were talking about

447
00:26:22,413 --> 00:26:26,394
earlier, where we sow and harvest.

448
00:26:27,193 --> 00:26:30,874
There's a lower layer,
yes, the groundwater.

449
00:26:30,973 --> 00:26:32,433
There's an even lower layer.

450
00:26:32,993 --> 00:26:38,533
All these layers studied by geodesy
and known to geodesists.

451
00:26:38,834 --> 00:26:39,054
Good.

452
00:26:39,374 --> 00:26:40,473
Geology.

453
00:26:41,253 --> 00:26:45,793
No, geodesy, which actually
deals with the structure of the earth.

454
00:26:45,874 --> 00:26:46,054
OK.

455
00:26:46,433 --> 00:26:47,793
With the layers of earth.

456
00:26:48,614 --> 00:26:51,274
And at the very bottom are the tectonic plates.

457
00:26:53,769 --> 00:26:59,009
Earthquakes are explained by
collisions between tectonic plates,

458
00:26:59,569 --> 00:27:05,389
or by their friction, by one of them
shifting higher or lower.

459
00:27:05,710 --> 00:27:09,569
Tectonic plates that are
continental, they're at the scale

460
00:27:09,609 --> 00:27:12,109
of continents, very deep.

461
00:27:12,150 --> 00:27:12,830
We don't know them.

462
00:27:13,410 --> 00:27:18,150
Only those who
work in this science

463
00:27:20,394 --> 00:27:28,034
of earthquakes can predict one, sadly,
only a few minutes in advance.

464
00:27:28,834 --> 00:27:34,953
So now I want to talk about the tectonic
plates the Romanian state rests on.

465
00:27:36,374 --> 00:27:40,173
There are two tectonic plates dating back to

466
00:27:42,213 --> 00:27:43,134
antiquity.

467
00:27:44,653 --> 00:27:47,993
In Ancient Greece people
talked about cosmopolitans.

468
00:27:48,874 --> 00:27:53,953
Cosmopolitan comes from cosmos, which
in ancient Greek meant universe,

469
00:27:54,594 --> 00:27:56,474
and polis, which meant city-state.

470
00:27:57,134 --> 00:28:00,574
So the cosmopolitan was
a citizen of the universe.

471
00:28:01,554 --> 00:28:05,173
He wasn't tied to a particular
state, to Athens, to Sparta.

472
00:28:06,153 --> 00:28:06,733
To a group.

473
00:28:06,894 --> 00:28:07,574
To a group.

474
00:28:08,134 --> 00:28:12,274
No, he was universal,
a universal citizen.

475
00:28:13,074 --> 00:28:20,514
He shared certain universal human
values, but he didn't feel

476
00:28:20,574 --> 00:28:22,193
obliged to any particular group.

477
00:28:22,653 --> 00:28:24,634
The way it is here, for
example, in rural areas.

478
00:28:24,673 --> 00:28:26,814
"Hey, cousin, how can you not help me?

479
00:28:27,193 --> 00:28:28,013
We're family."

480
00:28:29,433 --> 00:28:34,374
Or, "Hey neighbor, can't you
give me some too? We're neighbors."

481
00:28:34,993 --> 00:28:41,453
So there's this source of
moral obligations, which is the bond

482
00:28:41,513 --> 00:28:44,573
of kinship or of neighborliness.

483
00:28:44,933 --> 00:28:45,173
No.

484
00:28:45,473 --> 00:28:49,933
For a cosmopolitan: are you
honest, are you worthy?

485
00:28:50,074 --> 00:28:51,433
I don't care who you belong to.

486
00:28:52,534 --> 00:28:55,534
Are you logical and rational? You're human.

487
00:28:56,013 --> 00:28:57,233
Man is a rational being.

488
00:28:57,854 --> 00:29:03,334
Are you cooperative, benevolent
and open to others?

489
00:29:03,374 --> 00:29:04,433
Yes, you're human.

490
00:29:05,673 --> 00:29:07,533
I can cooperate with you.

491
00:29:07,894 --> 00:29:09,894
So these are universal human values.

492
00:29:09,933 --> 00:29:13,233
I don't care about your traditions
and how you happened to live

493
00:29:13,574 --> 00:29:14,994
and what you think is normal.

494
00:29:15,554 --> 00:29:17,454
That's what universalism means.

495
00:29:17,493 --> 00:29:24,553
But look, this struggle has been
fought many times in different

496
00:29:24,673 --> 00:29:29,394
eras, and I want to read a few facts
for those who are watching us.

497
00:29:29,593 --> 00:29:32,933
What universalism means,
what particularism means.

498
00:29:33,973 --> 00:29:38,333
Universalist culture accepts and serves
all newcomers equally,

499
00:29:38,733 --> 00:29:43,394
whether they want citizenship or just
a job or a higher income.

500
00:29:44,673 --> 00:29:48,693
That's what Europe does, and that's why
it's criticized by the likes of Donald Trump.

501
00:29:49,354 --> 00:29:50,894
"Europe is outdated.

502
00:29:51,013 --> 00:29:53,633
Look what it's doing on immigration.

503
00:29:53,773 --> 00:29:56,193
I'm against them.

504
00:29:56,733 --> 00:29:57,553
I build walls."

505
00:29:59,433 --> 00:30:03,374
Orbán's Hungary did the same,
following Trump's model.

506
00:30:04,145 --> 00:30:09,685
No, Europe and those in Brussels and
Mrs. Merkel and so on have

507
00:30:09,726 --> 00:30:14,946
followed those fundamental values
Europe has stood on since forever.

508
00:30:15,806 --> 00:30:21,246
Greco-Roman culture
and the Judeo-Christian religion.

509
00:30:22,266 --> 00:30:22,806
Notice.

510
00:30:23,165 --> 00:30:27,165
So that means: reach out
your hand to your neighbor.

511
00:30:28,026 --> 00:30:29,246
Help your neighbor.

512
00:30:29,806 --> 00:30:34,145
When the disciples ask, "Teacher,
who is our neighbor?", it's

513
00:30:34,266 --> 00:30:36,345
your fellow man who is in need.

514
00:30:37,066 --> 00:30:38,365
And so you take him in.

515
00:30:38,965 --> 00:30:44,885
You provide him a certain... You put up a filter
so not every terrorist gets into your country.

516
00:30:44,965 --> 00:30:46,085
That's the key here, the filter.

517
00:30:46,326 --> 00:30:46,986
I agree.

518
00:30:47,405 --> 00:30:51,625
Filters that were more or less
effective, but the attitude

519
00:30:51,726 --> 00:30:54,066
is this universalist one.

520
00:30:54,365 --> 00:30:58,905
Then, the sheer variety
of ethnic groups that come to America

521
00:30:58,945 --> 00:31:03,945
and live prosperously, mostly under
the authority of the law, is a phenomenon

522
00:31:04,026 --> 00:31:05,746
unprecedented in human history.

523
00:31:06,226 --> 00:31:10,186
They went there, and because
there's a strong state and laws

524
00:31:10,246 --> 00:31:14,146
that are respected, they managed to
build a nation, the American nation.

525
00:31:14,486 --> 00:31:16,026
But they come from different peoples.

526
00:31:16,526 --> 00:31:20,286
That's what the force of law means,
which is universal, because

527
00:31:20,326 --> 00:31:22,165
it's the same for everyone.

528
00:31:23,145 --> 00:31:27,806
A universalist culture takes every vote
into account, even if it can't

529
00:31:27,826 --> 00:31:30,546
make every vote count.

530
00:31:31,066 --> 00:31:35,385
For it, free and fair
elections are crucial. That's

531
00:31:35,405 --> 00:31:37,405
what it means to be a universalist.

532
00:31:38,326 --> 00:31:41,486
It supports and promotes
the rule of law.

533
00:31:41,605 --> 00:31:42,086
Naturally.

534
00:31:42,546 --> 00:31:47,346
The great strength of American universalism
is its tolerance for diversity.

535
00:31:47,965 --> 00:31:52,685
Hence, you know, the theory of tolerance,
the theory of affirmative action,

536
00:31:53,306 --> 00:31:55,286
the theory of political correctness.

537
00:31:55,685 --> 00:31:57,425
Actually, it's a social correctness.

538
00:31:57,925 --> 00:32:00,725
I'm fair to you,
however different you are.

539
00:32:01,645 --> 00:32:02,965
You're a human being just like me.

540
00:32:03,726 --> 00:32:04,226
That's right.

541
00:32:04,266 --> 00:32:04,605
Good.

542
00:32:04,645 --> 00:32:08,245
The problem is that pendulum, which
sometimes swung a bit too far.

543
00:32:08,246 --> 00:32:11,626
Yes, the excesses, but as a value
system, this is where it comes from.

544
00:32:12,246 --> 00:32:15,526
When it's taken too far,
the following happens.

545
00:32:16,006 --> 00:32:20,166
There are entire subjects and areas
of cultural interest that are

546
00:32:20,226 --> 00:32:22,166
particularist by definition.

547
00:32:22,846 --> 00:32:26,006
The arts, beliefs, religious practices.

548
00:32:26,286 --> 00:32:27,246
So what do you do with those?

549
00:32:27,326 --> 00:32:30,046
Do you subject them to laws, standardize them?

550
00:32:30,726 --> 00:32:32,026
Well, art isn't science.

551
00:32:32,965 --> 00:32:38,025
Art doesn't represent a universal law,
like the law of universal gravitation.

552
00:32:38,086 --> 00:32:38,306
No.

553
00:32:38,766 --> 00:32:43,266
Art comes and explains to me the unique,
unrepeatable experience of a person.

554
00:32:43,625 --> 00:32:43,865
Mm-hmm.

555
00:32:44,546 --> 00:32:48,885
That character is the embodiment
of a unique and

556
00:32:48,945 --> 00:32:50,545
unrepeatable human condition.

557
00:32:50,965 --> 00:32:52,085
There's no other one.

558
00:32:52,726 --> 00:32:57,146
So in art I'm no longer looking for rules,
I'm no longer looking for the universal,

559
00:32:57,226 --> 00:33:00,366
I'm looking for the particular, the specific.

560
00:33:01,145 --> 00:33:04,786
There, I gave an example so people
understand why universalist

561
00:33:04,846 --> 00:33:08,905
culture also has its disadvantages.

562
00:33:08,965 --> 00:33:11,525
It overlooks such phenomena.

563
00:33:11,945 --> 00:33:14,805
Another bad thing.

564
00:33:15,266 --> 00:33:18,846
American universalism, for example,
and the general enthusiasm for

565
00:33:19,385 --> 00:33:25,450
simple moral formulas led
to televangelism. A kind of religion

566
00:33:25,529 --> 00:33:31,388
via... Teleshopping ...yes, and to
fundamentalist dogmas, to what

567
00:33:31,430 --> 00:33:32,910
is happening now around Trump.

568
00:33:33,490 --> 00:33:41,970
There are such things, negative
reactions to universalism, and

569
00:33:42,009 --> 00:33:46,609
finally, stereotyping: the universal
criterion is nowhere more evident

570
00:33:46,670 --> 00:33:48,330
than in beauty pageants.

571
00:33:48,930 --> 00:33:53,190
You'd see the committee on un-American
activities that set the standard.

572
00:33:53,829 --> 00:33:57,250
Have you noticed how
much alike the Miss

573
00:33:57,369 --> 00:34:00,750
America winners look, year after year?

574
00:34:01,549 --> 00:34:08,529
It's a model of beauty that's
imposed as universal.

575
00:34:08,610 --> 00:34:11,829
Whereas people are
particular and individual.

576
00:34:12,949 --> 00:34:15,009
I see this reaching us too.

577
00:34:15,630 --> 00:34:20,550
You see that a lot of people try
to reach a certain standard of

578
00:34:20,610 --> 00:34:24,289
beauty which, on the other hand, is
quite far from their own features.

579
00:34:24,650 --> 00:34:25,889
Yes, they pay a lot of money.

580
00:34:26,210 --> 00:34:27,750
To change their features.

581
00:34:27,789 --> 00:34:28,009
Yes.

582
00:34:28,809 --> 00:34:34,570
Usually, the current model is
this: big eyes, sensual lips.

583
00:34:34,909 --> 00:34:40,630
No, hypertrophied lips— well—
hypertrophic... hyper, yes, which is

584
00:34:40,690 --> 00:34:45,869
why they become monstrous, but
the model, the gestalt, is this:

585
00:34:46,530 --> 00:34:51,810
Big eyes, full lips
and— Long, big hair.

586
00:34:51,929 --> 00:34:52,949
Long, big hair, yes.

587
00:34:53,009 --> 00:34:53,829
Kimberly Guilfoyle.

588
00:34:54,030 --> 00:34:54,989
Yes, yes, yes, exactly.

589
00:34:55,170 --> 00:34:57,190
Oh, haven't you seen Kimberly Guilfoyle?

590
00:34:57,349 --> 00:34:59,150
So if you look at her,
you see this prototype.

591
00:34:59,710 --> 00:34:59,889
Yes.

592
00:35:00,630 --> 00:35:01,090
Thank you.

593
00:35:01,929 --> 00:35:06,190
Now let's move on to particularism,
because particularism is interesting here,

594
00:35:06,210 --> 00:35:10,769
and what kind of psychological
consciousness a particularist has.

595
00:35:10,829 --> 00:35:15,590
He doesn't see the things that
bind us, the things we share;

596
00:35:15,650 --> 00:35:17,150
he sees the things that set us apart.

597
00:35:17,750 --> 00:35:19,389
Those are what interest him.

598
00:35:20,070 --> 00:35:23,690
That is, a different interest in
the things that make us distinct.

599
00:35:24,050 --> 00:35:27,070
And this is where— I'll explain in a moment.

600
00:35:27,909 --> 00:35:31,489
It's a defensive reaction against
a vulgar universalist doctrine,

601
00:35:31,610 --> 00:35:34,528
which—as you said—made the
pendulum swing the other way.

602
00:35:35,836 --> 00:35:40,396
A particularist culture celebrates
what is unique and incomparable about

603
00:35:40,456 --> 00:35:42,538
people, situations and events.

604
00:35:43,177 --> 00:35:45,657
It's oriented toward exceptional tastes.

605
00:35:46,257 --> 00:35:47,456
That's particularism.

606
00:35:48,117 --> 00:35:51,956
If we feared the unknown,
the never-seen and the original,

607
00:35:52,038 --> 00:35:53,177
we could no longer create.

608
00:35:53,697 --> 00:35:59,956
So we are creative beings
because we also take into account

609
00:36:00,018 --> 00:36:03,798
what is unique and surprising.

610
00:36:04,436 --> 00:36:08,976
To be alive is to escape uniformity
and be a person like no other.

611
00:36:09,577 --> 00:36:14,677
They're the ones rejecting school uniforms
today, nowadays, in Romania.

612
00:36:15,817 --> 00:36:20,538
They want the specific,
the unique, the personal.

613
00:36:20,597 --> 00:36:23,277
Shall we talk for two minutes
about this school uniform thing?

614
00:36:23,777 --> 00:36:23,956
Yes.

615
00:36:24,956 --> 00:36:26,077
Shall we take a detour here?

616
00:36:26,177 --> 00:36:26,976
Yes, a detour.

617
00:36:28,038 --> 00:36:30,538
How do you see this whole
dispute over school uniforms?

618
00:36:31,976 --> 00:36:38,876
It's also a choice of values,
and these are, in

619
00:36:38,956 --> 00:36:44,336
fact, distant consequences of
these orientations we're talking about.

620
00:36:45,257 --> 00:36:50,936
School uniforms are wanted by the
universalist, who wants equality

621
00:36:50,956 --> 00:36:55,617
before the law, equality before the
school, before the principal, before

622
00:36:55,657 --> 00:36:57,217
the teacher, before the form teacher.

623
00:36:58,476 --> 00:37:04,637
I don't come in with watches and
bracelets to show off my social status.

624
00:37:04,856 --> 00:37:09,336
Everyone needs to be equal
before the law, before authority,

625
00:37:09,376 --> 00:37:14,077
before the form teacher, before the
teacher, before the other classmates.

626
00:37:14,757 --> 00:37:19,416
Because the economic, financial
differences we bring with us

627
00:37:19,456 --> 00:37:24,597
from home can no longer be flaunted,
can no longer be shown to others.

628
00:37:24,956 --> 00:37:29,717
The uniform makes us equal, it
doesn't truly make us all the same.

629
00:37:29,757 --> 00:37:29,996
Why?

630
00:37:30,137 --> 00:37:40,077
Because there's always, as you
know very well, that je ne sais quoi.

631
00:37:40,157 --> 00:37:40,936
Je ne sais quoi.

632
00:37:41,257 --> 00:37:42,038
Je ne sais quoi.

633
00:37:42,336 --> 00:37:43,317
That was the expression.

634
00:37:44,577 --> 00:37:48,936
It's something of ours, of each
of us, that makes us— something

635
00:37:49,577 --> 00:37:52,137
that makes us unique.

636
00:37:52,657 --> 00:38:00,617
So, however much— the uniform
equalizes you, but doesn't standardize you.

637
00:38:01,416 --> 00:38:03,916
And those who are for
uniforms know this.

638
00:38:03,976 --> 00:38:08,456
It's not so serious that we'd say,
"Look, we're standardized, we're robots."

639
00:38:08,677 --> 00:38:08,916
No.

640
00:38:09,916 --> 00:38:09,956
Um...

641
00:38:12,018 --> 00:38:19,038
The others are in the particularist
paradigm, saying that we must

642
00:38:19,117 --> 00:38:25,637
encourage the individual's uniqueness,
their personality, their individuation,

643
00:38:25,737 --> 00:38:31,337
as psychologists say, meaning letting
individuals express themselves as much

644
00:38:31,398 --> 00:38:34,698
as possible as unique,
unrepeatable individuals.

645
00:38:35,458 --> 00:38:41,617
But this often leads to a kind of
bracketing-off of society, of

646
00:38:42,057 --> 00:38:48,057
the social group, of social norms,
of the rules for living with others.

647
00:38:48,717 --> 00:38:56,357
So, you see, there's always
this problem of the

648
00:38:58,458 --> 00:38:59,157
pendulum.

649
00:38:59,237 --> 00:39:02,697
Not swinging to the extremes,
neither one way nor the other.

650
00:39:03,458 --> 00:39:06,918
What happens to particularism
when it's taken too far?

651
00:39:07,358 --> 00:39:10,178
And here we're talking about
that unhealthy nationalism.

652
00:39:10,697 --> 00:39:12,437
But you didn't give me a—
you didn't give me a conclusion.

653
00:39:12,918 --> 00:39:15,798
Do you think it's a good idea
to introduce uniforms or not?

654
00:39:15,898 --> 00:39:22,718
Right now I think it's a good
idea, because the chaos

655
00:39:22,757 --> 00:39:27,157
Romanian schools have descended into
is so great that it's an

656
00:39:27,197 --> 00:39:29,498
attempt to restore order.

657
00:39:30,577 --> 00:39:32,597
Some order, a certain order.

658
00:39:32,657 --> 00:39:35,337
You know there aren't
many schools doing this.

659
00:39:35,617 --> 00:39:39,817
What I find surprising, though, I don't
know if you've noticed, it's mostly

660
00:39:39,858 --> 00:39:44,538
private schools that bring in uniforms
so they can truly do education.

661
00:39:44,898 --> 00:39:48,478
What's more, the most expensive schools
in Britain, because I did a bit of

662
00:39:48,538 --> 00:39:53,077
research— They have uniforms— They
have— Superb ones, not just uniforms.

663
00:39:53,697 --> 00:39:58,417
I heard an argument from students who
protested, saying: don't make us

664
00:39:58,438 --> 00:40:01,778
come in uniforms, because if you want
to talk about bullying, the competition

665
00:40:01,838 --> 00:40:03,378
will just move from clothes to jewelry.

666
00:40:03,717 --> 00:40:04,998
The British know this.

667
00:40:05,157 --> 00:40:06,277
Jewelry isn't allowed either.

668
00:40:06,478 --> 00:40:06,838
Exactly.

669
00:40:06,878 --> 00:40:07,958
No wristwatches either.

670
00:40:08,018 --> 00:40:12,557
No watches, no jewelry—
even phones are locked away at the door.

671
00:40:12,597 --> 00:40:12,717
Yes.

672
00:40:13,237 --> 00:40:18,917
And if you see the dorm rooms where
the children of lords and the children

673
00:40:18,978 --> 00:40:24,197
of kings go to school, they look worse
than the Poli and Regie dorms here.

674
00:40:24,737 --> 00:40:25,917
Yes, I've seen them.

675
00:40:26,257 --> 00:40:27,557
I've seen the dorms— You've seen them?

676
00:40:27,557 --> 00:40:28,378
At Cambridge, yes.

677
00:40:28,657 --> 00:40:33,877
Modest entrances, narrow
hallways, small, dark rooms.

678
00:40:34,438 --> 00:40:34,918
That's right.

679
00:40:35,458 --> 00:40:38,898
But— No air conditioning, many of
them with toilets down the hall.

680
00:40:39,777 --> 00:40:43,637
So the British we were talking about
at the start understood this.

681
00:40:43,677 --> 00:40:44,637
What don't our people get?

682
00:40:44,697 --> 00:40:46,177
What don't we get about uniforms?

683
00:40:46,197 --> 00:40:47,817
We have— no, we have a different history.

684
00:40:48,498 --> 00:40:55,577
Don't forget, the two of us were
talking before the show about

685
00:40:55,637 --> 00:41:01,826
the round table, why it's more democratic
than a table that has one side for

686
00:41:01,925 --> 00:41:05,545
the presiding panel and one side for
those who must listen and take notes.

687
00:41:06,365 --> 00:41:08,186
The round table is more democratic.

688
00:41:08,726 --> 00:41:10,405
It was invented by the English.

689
00:41:10,626 --> 00:41:10,846
Uh-huh.

690
00:41:11,465 --> 00:41:12,846
The Knights of the Round Table.

691
00:41:13,826 --> 00:41:17,925
In England, there

692
00:41:20,054 --> 00:41:24,213
emerged and took hold
the cult of Robin Hood.

693
00:41:24,634 --> 00:41:25,673
Who was Robin Hood?

694
00:41:26,373 --> 00:41:31,973
A hero who fought against
privilege and abuse of power.

695
00:41:32,673 --> 00:41:33,813
The Sheriff of Nottingham.

696
00:41:34,733 --> 00:41:39,873
He's the popular hero, a hero
of the English people, because he

697
00:41:39,893 --> 00:41:42,493
stands against abuse and against power.

698
00:41:42,954 --> 00:41:48,314
That's when, alongside the House of
Lords, the House of Commons was founded,

699
00:41:48,753 --> 00:41:53,514
which represents the people
who come from below, from the grass.

700
00:41:54,713 --> 00:41:56,453
That's also why it's green.

701
00:41:57,494 --> 00:42:02,814
The House of Lords has all
its upholstery in imperial red.

702
00:42:04,153 --> 00:42:08,273
The House of Commons is in green,
it's the common folk there.

703
00:42:08,713 --> 00:42:12,974
So there's always a
democratic propensity.

704
00:42:13,594 --> 00:42:16,354
That's what allowed
Karl Marx, who was hounded by

705
00:42:16,414 --> 00:42:20,414
every regime in Europe, to
grow old and die in London.

706
00:42:21,134 --> 00:42:27,514
It allowed Sigmund Freud, who was
considered a scoundrel blowing

707
00:42:27,614 --> 00:42:34,494
bourgeois morality sky-high with his
subconscious, his libido, his obsession with

708
00:42:34,554 --> 00:42:37,074
sex, to grow old and die in London.

709
00:42:38,134 --> 00:42:44,333
Salman Rushdie, who is
hunted by the— Islamic Revolution.

710
00:42:44,534 --> 00:42:44,814
Sorry?

711
00:42:45,034 --> 00:42:45,474
Islamic.

712
00:42:45,813 --> 00:42:52,333
Islamic, to be protected for years
by the British state and to live

713
00:42:52,393 --> 00:43:01,693
there at the expense of the British,
who took him in, in hiding.

714
00:43:02,074 --> 00:43:07,813
No one was ever told where he lived,
and I think even his name, his identity

715
00:43:07,873 --> 00:43:12,293
was changed, to protect him from
the long arm of the Islamic Revolution.

716
00:43:12,793 --> 00:43:14,554
You see what Great Britain means?

717
00:43:14,974 --> 00:43:21,014
And then we're surprised that they ban
watches and jewelry in schools.

718
00:43:21,454 --> 00:43:23,894
They have this deeply humane mentality.

719
00:43:24,313 --> 00:43:26,493
They have rules for shoes too, and— Yes.

720
00:43:26,914 --> 00:43:28,414
Of equality among people.

721
00:43:29,173 --> 00:43:30,713
They've always had that.

722
00:43:31,393 --> 00:43:36,813
So— But equality without sliding into
communism, because that again is a

723
00:43:36,893 --> 00:43:43,033
remarkable form of balance, because
we all see that this standardization,

724
00:43:43,173 --> 00:43:46,353
the uniform leads to uniformity, and
there'd be the risk of going back to

725
00:43:46,393 --> 00:43:48,833
communism, when in fact that's not it.

726
00:43:49,213 --> 00:43:49,753
Not at all.

727
00:43:50,054 --> 00:43:53,414
You get communism when you say "shut up,
don't speak unless spoken to"

728
00:43:53,893 --> 00:43:55,433
when a student wants to ask a question.

729
00:43:55,833 --> 00:43:56,713
Well, why is he here?

730
00:43:56,773 --> 00:43:57,594
Isn't he here to learn?

731
00:43:58,313 --> 00:44:01,574
Isn't a question a
tool for learning?

732
00:44:02,525 --> 00:44:08,905
So the moment you violate
his right to education and his right

733
00:44:08,965 --> 00:44:11,585
to emancipation, that's
when you're a communist.

734
00:44:11,706 --> 00:44:16,865
Not when you make them wear uniforms
which, between us, where there are

735
00:44:16,945 --> 00:44:22,826
private schools and the students come
from well-off families, are splendid.

736
00:44:23,565 --> 00:44:26,445
They're beautiful uniforms,
made of expensive fabric.

737
00:44:26,965 --> 00:44:32,545
I don't know if the Romanian state can
afford that, or certain public schools

738
00:44:33,045 --> 00:44:37,525
in Romania, but— But I don't think it's
cheaper now for those who don't wear

739
00:44:37,565 --> 00:44:42,365
uniforms; even if they dress at H&M
and Bershka, it still costs more, because

740
00:44:42,405 --> 00:44:43,485
that stuff isn't durable.

741
00:44:43,525 --> 00:44:47,306
Those kids grow, wear things out,
rub them thin, and in the end still get

742
00:44:47,326 --> 00:44:48,726
pulled into this spiral of competition.

743
00:44:48,746 --> 00:44:49,485
Yes, I agree.

744
00:44:49,766 --> 00:44:54,425
Because, yes, the trousers might be
cheaper, but they'll spend a lot

745
00:44:54,465 --> 00:44:57,585
on shoes, because some trend pops up.

746
00:44:57,644 --> 00:45:00,605
There were Air Max, now I don't
know what it is. Balenciaga.

747
00:45:00,965 --> 00:45:02,684
You see some kid coming in with
some thing on his wrist.

748
00:45:02,684 --> 00:45:09,065
I mean, in adolescence, competition
among kids and social positioning are

749
00:45:09,085 --> 00:45:10,445
more important than survival.

750
00:45:10,485 --> 00:45:15,306
We've talked about these things before,
and I think that even if I've dwelt

751
00:45:15,365 --> 00:45:19,545
a bit on this, it says something about
our adolescence as a society.

752
00:45:19,585 --> 00:45:26,144
Yes, we're infantile, and
we don't see solutions beyond

753
00:45:26,286 --> 00:45:27,706
our own personal problem.

754
00:45:28,865 --> 00:45:32,806
When particularism is taken
much too far— because you

755
00:45:33,125 --> 00:45:37,684
sensed it very well when you wanted
to take this detour, it's a

756
00:45:37,746 --> 00:45:42,566
classic example of a clash between
universalism and particularism—

757
00:45:43,346 --> 00:45:47,786
it becomes hostile to human rights and
to universal equality of rights.

758
00:45:49,161 --> 00:45:49,661
It's hostile.

759
00:45:49,862 --> 00:45:51,300
No, they don't like that.

760
00:45:52,161 --> 00:45:56,442
Without the sanction of legality,
particularism often comes down

761
00:45:56,481 --> 00:46:00,902
to power and coercion,
using intimidation, mystification,

762
00:46:01,021 --> 00:46:03,121
complicity and conspiracy.

763
00:46:03,681 --> 00:46:07,401
Does that seem easy to
recognize in Romanian politics?

764
00:46:07,442 --> 00:46:09,882
I've been itching to say that
for half an hour, that's them.

765
00:46:10,121 --> 00:46:10,320
Yes.

766
00:46:10,760 --> 00:46:16,561
Next, the products of particularist
culture are nationalism, tribalism,

767
00:46:17,240 --> 00:46:24,061
this demagogic super-patriotism and
the pull toward ethnic identity.

768
00:46:24,681 --> 00:46:26,800
Instead of having a civic identity.

769
00:46:27,561 --> 00:46:31,041
Saying: we are Romanians who
have the following political project.

770
00:46:31,840 --> 00:46:40,260
We're all harnessed to bringing Romania
among the mid-developed countries, into

771
00:46:40,501 --> 00:46:42,820
the semi-periphery of
the global economic empire.

772
00:46:42,882 --> 00:46:48,221
To leave the periphery and enter
the semi-periphery by the year 2050.

773
00:46:48,521 --> 00:46:48,701
OK.

774
00:46:49,300 --> 00:46:55,641
From then on, in 2050, we can aim—
we'll be able to aim to enter the core

775
00:46:55,882 --> 00:47:01,162
of the economic empire, to be
among the most developed countries.

776
00:47:01,221 --> 00:47:02,882
No, we have no project at all.

777
00:47:03,340 --> 00:47:06,740
For 35 years we've been
without a project for society.

778
00:47:07,121 --> 00:47:10,820
We tear each other apart
over very small stakes and over

779
00:47:10,882 --> 00:47:12,702
some kind of group narcissism.

780
00:47:14,021 --> 00:47:21,181
Right, what else: whatever is considered
sacred and religious is defied by the great

781
00:47:21,260 --> 00:47:23,481
Satan, that is, by universalism.

782
00:47:23,760 --> 00:47:27,402
Universalism is seen as satanic.

783
00:47:27,800 --> 00:47:28,641
Meaning the globalists.

784
00:47:28,922 --> 00:47:31,840
The globalists who intend
to turn the world

785
00:47:31,902 --> 00:47:33,382
into a fast-food empire.

786
00:47:34,021 --> 00:47:41,601
They want to standardize us, to
have us all eat fast food, to

787
00:47:41,661 --> 00:47:43,901
change our sex, and so on.

788
00:47:44,021 --> 00:47:44,481
I recognize that.

789
00:47:45,260 --> 00:47:48,081
The most dangerous is
particularism dressed up as

790
00:47:48,221 --> 00:47:50,340
universalism, as Ceaușism was.

791
00:47:51,336 --> 00:47:55,936
Ceaușescu constantly promoted
particularist values.

792
00:47:56,737 --> 00:48:02,496
That's why it's called— it was
named with an

793
00:48:03,376 --> 00:48:08,197
expression that contains
a contradiction in terms.

794
00:48:08,956 --> 00:48:11,856
Yes, it's called
contradictio in adjecto.

795
00:48:14,177 --> 00:48:17,157
National socialism or
socialist nationalism.

796
00:48:17,737 --> 00:48:19,856
Socialism is a universal doctrine.

797
00:48:20,697 --> 00:48:24,516
I don't know if everyone knows that
Marx and Engels' Communist Manifesto

798
00:48:24,538 --> 00:48:29,856
ends with this call:
"Proletarians of all countries, unite!"

799
00:48:29,916 --> 00:48:34,936
They noticed that there would be
a globalization of capital, that

800
00:48:34,996 --> 00:48:36,836
its force would become invincible.

801
00:48:37,297 --> 00:48:41,496
So if you want to change anything,
to overthrow capital in order to

802
00:48:41,876 --> 00:48:48,416
express your human personality, to
overcome the human alienation specific to

803
00:48:48,856 --> 00:48:52,896
capitalist society, you must
unite internationally, because

804
00:48:52,936 --> 00:48:55,336
otherwise you have no chance of beating it.

805
00:48:55,856 --> 00:48:56,476
They didn't unite.

806
00:48:56,757 --> 00:49:00,297
On the contrary, in World War I
proletarians from different countries

807
00:49:00,356 --> 00:49:04,777
slaughtered one another, because they
fell prey to nationalist propaganda.

808
00:49:05,496 --> 00:49:08,516
There you go, that's how
it was in World War I.

809
00:49:08,836 --> 00:49:12,376
So this is socialism's weakness:
it drifts toward nationalism.

810
00:49:13,277 --> 00:49:20,157
Ceaușism was that, or Nazism,
but no, socialism is universalist.

811
00:49:20,996 --> 00:49:23,416
It doesn't drift toward
nationalism, quite the opposite.

812
00:49:23,797 --> 00:49:26,456
Except, what did Ceaușescu do?

813
00:49:26,817 --> 00:49:31,097
He legitimized himself through
a left-wing universalist discourse.

814
00:49:32,197 --> 00:49:40,057
"Brotherly countries, socialism this and that,
but let us have the freedom

815
00:49:40,117 --> 00:49:44,637
to apply revolutionary theory to
Romania's concrete conditions," and

816
00:49:44,657 --> 00:49:46,817
with that he slid into particularism.

817
00:49:47,817 --> 00:49:52,798
And in the name of this creative
development of revolutionary theory,

818
00:49:53,396 --> 00:49:58,157
Ceaușescu did whatever he wanted,
whatever he or his wife dreamed at night.

819
00:49:58,856 --> 00:50:02,057
They'd come and turn it into
a Central Committee decision.

820
00:50:02,577 --> 00:50:09,416
That was the essence of Ceaușescu's
totalitarianism: particularism taken to

821
00:50:09,476 --> 00:50:12,336
the extreme, but with a universalist face.

822
00:50:12,396 --> 00:50:12,836
Why?

823
00:50:13,177 --> 00:50:19,057
Because otherwise they'd have thrown him out
of the international communist movement,

824
00:50:19,157 --> 00:50:20,518
out of the workers' movement.

825
00:50:22,097 --> 00:50:25,177
With which he was already
in open conflict anyway.

826
00:50:25,757 --> 00:50:28,436
All the discussions he had
with Gorbachev and others— Yes.

827
00:50:30,677 --> 00:50:36,637
They sensed he was a nationalist,
closed in on himself, not thinking.

828
00:50:36,998 --> 00:50:41,998
The problem wasn't whether he tried
to promote Romania's interests, since

829
00:50:42,057 --> 00:50:44,438
everyone promoted their countries' interests.

830
00:50:45,418 --> 00:50:49,518
The problem was that they saw him as
closed-minded, unable to think

831
00:50:49,577 --> 00:50:51,918
beyond Romania's borders.

832
00:50:52,297 --> 00:50:55,017
Exactly what he did when
he paid off the foreign debt.

833
00:50:55,697 --> 00:50:57,777
He didn't realize, that's
as far as his mind went.

834
00:50:58,157 --> 00:51:03,096
He didn't realize that if he pulled out
of the international economic and financial

835
00:51:03,478 --> 00:51:09,498
mechanism, he'd no longer matter,
no one would take him seriously anymore,

836
00:51:10,157 --> 00:51:12,438
and they could even replace and kill him.

837
00:51:12,657 --> 00:51:13,518
That's exactly what happened.

838
00:51:14,257 --> 00:51:20,417
He paid with his life for a utopia: pulling
himself out of globalization, which

839
00:51:20,458 --> 00:51:22,378
means we all depend on one another.

840
00:51:23,038 --> 00:51:27,318
He pulled out and was left
without the Most Favored

841
00:51:27,358 --> 00:51:29,097
Nation clause from the Americans.

842
00:51:29,677 --> 00:51:32,817
He was left— he paid off
the debts, but at the cost

843
00:51:34,958 --> 00:51:39,697
of international isolation and
at the cost of misery at home.

844
00:51:40,297 --> 00:51:42,477
That whole decade of— Hunger.

845
00:51:42,737 --> 00:51:43,057
Of hunger.

846
00:51:43,077 --> 00:51:43,717
And darkness.

847
00:51:43,757 --> 00:51:44,018
Yes.

848
00:51:44,077 --> 00:51:44,597
And cold.

849
00:51:44,617 --> 00:51:45,117
And cold.

850
00:51:45,777 --> 00:51:46,997
And do you know what that led to?

851
00:51:47,157 --> 00:51:51,837
It led to a steep decline in
the ethos, the morality of Romanians.

852
00:51:52,518 --> 00:51:57,157
Because in a society where
the main goal is survival,

853
00:51:57,757 --> 00:51:59,477
the good is no longer the moral good.

854
00:51:59,637 --> 00:52:00,757
The good becomes the useful.

855
00:52:01,657 --> 00:52:06,458
Useful or not useful, does it serve
survival, and that's how we got to—

856
00:52:06,597 --> 00:52:09,478
Well, seriously now, what was my dad
supposed to do, go and read

857
00:52:09,958 --> 00:52:11,498
doctrines or bring home milk?

858
00:52:11,938 --> 00:52:12,818
He had to bring home milk.

859
00:52:13,657 --> 00:52:15,317
He'd jump the fence, go
and get hold of some milk.

860
00:52:15,338 --> 00:52:15,838
Yes, yes.

861
00:52:16,418 --> 00:52:16,998
A concrete case.

862
00:52:19,260 --> 00:52:20,641
You know what I used to do in Brașov?

863
00:52:21,360 --> 00:52:24,740
I'd get up at four in the morning, often
I wouldn't even go to bed, I worked,

864
00:52:25,641 --> 00:52:27,442
I wrote, I had a lot of reading to do.

865
00:52:28,280 --> 00:52:30,240
I was a teaching assistant
at the University of Brașov.

866
00:52:31,081 --> 00:52:32,340
I had two kids to raise.

867
00:52:33,221 --> 00:52:37,721
The two liters of milk weren't
enough for me, the ones that

868
00:52:37,760 --> 00:52:39,942
Ceaușescu gave me through rationing.

869
00:52:40,601 --> 00:52:42,481
Rationing of consumption.

870
00:52:42,521 --> 00:52:43,400
So what did I do?

871
00:52:43,920 --> 00:52:48,942
I'd get up at four in the morning and go
stand in line, one of those lines, in the dark,

872
00:52:49,041 --> 00:52:52,420
like that, we were like ghosts
in the pitch black.

873
00:52:54,141 --> 00:52:57,541
In winter, with temperatures
of minus twenty degrees

874
00:52:57,581 --> 00:52:59,860
in Brașov, what did I do?

875
00:53:00,121 --> 00:53:07,360
I had a coat, and over that coat
a wider sheepskin jacket, to hold out.

876
00:53:07,442 --> 00:53:13,961
A fur hat pulled down over my forehead,
and I'd stand in line so I'd have time

877
00:53:14,001 --> 00:53:15,880
to queue once more for another two liters.

878
00:53:16,702 --> 00:53:20,102
Whoever managed to queue
twice was a big shot.

879
00:53:20,501 --> 00:53:27,101
I'd come home happy with four liters
of milk, take a shower and head

880
00:53:27,141 --> 00:53:28,820
up University Hill to teach.

881
00:53:29,740 --> 00:53:31,621
That's how I raised my two kids.

882
00:53:32,740 --> 00:53:36,840
Getting up at four in the morning
and standing in the freezing cold until

883
00:53:36,880 --> 00:53:39,840
the grocery store opened at six.

884
00:53:41,240 --> 00:53:46,081
Did that cure you of that nationalism
and that socialism?

885
00:53:46,521 --> 00:53:47,320
You know what? No.

886
00:53:47,461 --> 00:53:52,101
Nationalism— Ceaușism was
nationalist, not socialist.

887
00:53:52,840 --> 00:53:57,621
The problem is that, once again,
socialism is universalist.

888
00:53:58,340 --> 00:54:00,221
The problem is that...

889
00:54:02,813 --> 00:54:07,413
I, like others, had come to
want anyone at all to come, even

890
00:54:07,472 --> 00:54:09,614
the Russians, to rid us of Ceaușescu.

891
00:54:11,594 --> 00:54:15,854
So it was utterly anti-patriotic.

892
00:54:16,134 --> 00:54:25,554
Ceaușism, this, as that guy
here used to say, the patriotism

893
00:54:26,014 --> 00:54:29,534
of parades, super-patriotism, right?

894
00:54:30,134 --> 00:54:35,753
Super-patriotism was
actually anti-patriotic, because

895
00:54:35,753 --> 00:54:39,972
it made Romanians want anything,
just to be rid of Ceaușescu.

896
00:54:40,773 --> 00:54:42,193
Sounds very familiar.

897
00:54:43,034 --> 00:54:44,273
Let anyone come now.

898
00:54:44,673 --> 00:54:44,833
Yes.

899
00:54:45,534 --> 00:54:46,253
"Screw you all."

900
00:54:46,994 --> 00:54:48,094
It's one big "screw you all."

901
00:54:48,373 --> 00:54:53,233
Yes, that's right. And that's why Romanian
nationalists lean heavily toward Russia,

902
00:54:53,313 --> 00:54:58,614
because they see on the other side
the apparent source of their problems.

903
00:54:59,353 --> 00:55:01,313
Yes, they see what they see.

904
00:55:01,693 --> 00:55:07,134
You know how it is— I told you before
on your show, with the nearsighted man:

905
00:55:07,192 --> 00:55:11,972
if you show him the moon, he looks at
the finger, because he can't see that far.

906
00:55:12,733 --> 00:55:13,614
He sees the finger.

907
00:55:15,114 --> 00:55:16,614
There's such a thing as political myopia.

908
00:55:16,873 --> 00:55:19,373
It comes from our traditional
lack of political culture.

909
00:55:19,614 --> 00:55:23,313
We have no political culture, we
have plenty of political psychology.

910
00:55:23,393 --> 00:55:23,593
Uh-huh.

911
00:55:24,034 --> 00:55:31,514
Meaning political feelings, political
attitudes, by the bucketload,

912
00:55:31,614 --> 00:55:35,773
because that's what brings people out
into the streets, into University—

913
00:55:35,813 --> 00:55:39,413
Victoria Square, but not political culture.

914
00:55:39,452 --> 00:55:39,913
Why?

915
00:55:40,393 --> 00:55:41,033
No, Mr. Buhnici.

916
00:55:41,074 --> 00:55:44,514
Culture is defined as a system
of values and symbols,

917
00:55:45,594 --> 00:55:47,452
of rules and rituals.

918
00:55:48,153 --> 00:55:50,773
And that means political values.

919
00:55:51,373 --> 00:55:52,153
Those we don't have.

920
00:55:53,494 --> 00:55:57,653
We have no political culture, we have
only political psychology, and our

921
00:55:57,693 --> 00:55:59,153
reactions are at a psychological level.

922
00:56:00,134 --> 00:56:01,074
Mass psychology.

923
00:56:02,902 --> 00:56:06,521
Well, to have discussions like this,
we first need to meet our basic

924
00:56:06,581 --> 00:56:08,161
needs, but we've more or less met them.

925
00:56:08,601 --> 00:56:11,942
I expected that once we'd all met
our basic needs, since people no longer

926
00:56:11,981 --> 00:56:13,521
starve to death in Romania, Professor...

927
00:56:14,081 --> 00:56:14,521
They no longer do.

928
00:56:14,621 --> 00:56:17,141
...we'd have other discussions,
talk about new things.

929
00:56:18,202 --> 00:56:19,822
Well— Not about Ceaușism.

930
00:56:19,942 --> 00:56:22,862
The next thing is identity.

931
00:56:23,581 --> 00:56:24,541
How do we identify ourselves?

932
00:56:24,601 --> 00:56:27,021
Because without identity
we don't know who we are.

933
00:56:27,802 --> 00:56:28,662
It's a great need.

934
00:56:28,981 --> 00:56:33,942
You know very well that human nature has
needs that go beyond the basic ones,

935
00:56:34,581 --> 00:56:42,862
having to do with belonging, emotional
security—so not physical security.

936
00:56:43,382 --> 00:56:44,081
Having food to eat.

937
00:56:44,541 --> 00:56:46,541
It's emotional security,
feeling safe.

938
00:56:46,581 --> 00:56:47,621
What's the difference between them?

939
00:56:47,942 --> 00:56:48,521
Oh my!

940
00:56:48,961 --> 00:56:52,742
Well, why does Otilia
marry Pascalopol?

941
00:56:54,601 --> 00:57:00,801
Out of the need
for long-term security.

942
00:57:01,882 --> 00:57:05,961
Because Pascalopol had
economic and financial power.

943
00:57:06,501 --> 00:57:07,460
Felix was the one she loved.

944
00:57:07,802 --> 00:57:10,322
He was young, handsome, a medical student.

945
00:57:10,961 --> 00:57:16,342
He had a future, but for now he
didn't even have enough to live on.

946
00:57:16,702 --> 00:57:18,182
He was just a poor student.

947
00:57:19,322 --> 00:57:20,842
That's how nature selects.

948
00:57:21,222 --> 00:57:21,402
Yes.

949
00:57:21,981 --> 00:57:22,362
Unfortunately.

950
00:57:22,382 --> 00:57:24,822
Nature selects for
adaptability and viability.

951
00:57:24,882 --> 00:57:25,101
Yes.

952
00:57:25,842 --> 00:57:30,722
And medium- and long-term
security is fundamental.

953
00:57:31,242 --> 00:57:34,862
So when people lose
something like that, as is happening

954
00:57:34,902 --> 00:57:40,141
in Romanian society today, over
the last decade, people become— they

955
00:57:40,181 --> 00:57:46,021
retreat to the strong codes, and the strong
code is belonging to family and nation.

956
00:57:46,842 --> 00:57:51,602
That's what they were told for decades
through Sergiu Nicolaescu's films.

957
00:57:51,981 --> 00:57:53,181
Which are a construct.

958
00:57:53,661 --> 00:57:54,401
A construct.

959
00:57:54,641 --> 00:57:55,641
That is a construct.

960
00:57:56,001 --> 00:58:03,782
The Romanian nation as we see it, forgive
me, is a story, it's a legend.

961
00:58:04,081 --> 00:58:04,581
That's right.

962
00:58:04,902 --> 00:58:05,882
It's just a legend.

963
00:58:06,282 --> 00:58:10,802
We are a construct created here,
on the edge, the edge of empire,

964
00:58:11,222 --> 00:58:12,722
by Franco-British interests.

965
00:58:13,021 --> 00:58:13,922
Let me explain further.

966
00:58:14,902 --> 00:58:17,702
You know what I read recently?

967
00:58:18,742 --> 00:58:19,481
An anthropologist.

968
00:58:19,621 --> 00:58:22,561
Cultural anthropology is
a very interesting science.

969
00:58:23,101 --> 00:58:27,301
Even nostalgia for the past is really
nostalgia for a past that's

970
00:58:27,362 --> 00:58:29,762
gone. Well, but it never even existed.

971
00:58:31,081 --> 00:58:34,161
Sometimes it did, but it's gone,
it's been gone since you were a kid.

972
00:58:34,621 --> 00:58:36,161
But you have the nostalgia.

973
00:58:36,581 --> 00:58:39,382
When did you live best
in your whole life?

974
00:58:39,942 --> 00:58:40,141
Now.

975
00:58:41,121 --> 00:58:45,801
When was it ever better for you
than now, in any other way?

976
00:58:46,621 --> 00:58:50,521
Maybe in my student years, but because
I was a student, not because life was good.

977
00:58:51,717 --> 00:58:51,998
That's it.

978
00:58:52,157 --> 00:58:56,177
Because after that came a wave
of humiliations, like I was telling you.

979
00:58:56,637 --> 00:59:00,817
Standing in line from four in the
morning to raise two kids.

980
00:59:01,237 --> 00:59:03,697
I understand all of this
is called "passéism."

981
00:59:04,018 --> 00:59:05,278
Yes, yes.

982
00:59:05,458 --> 00:59:06,518
Turning back to the past.

983
00:59:06,898 --> 00:59:10,738
So I see three fundamental
attitudes in today's Romania.

984
00:59:11,938 --> 00:59:14,298
A passéist attitude, toward the past.

985
00:59:14,797 --> 00:59:17,597
And that past differs
from one group to another.

986
00:59:17,697 --> 00:59:25,777
Either Ceaușism, the Legionary Movement,
or the free Dacians—each has its own image

987
00:59:25,858 --> 00:59:28,958
of Romania's golden age.

988
00:59:29,336 --> 00:59:34,277
Order, we had jobs, I was
young, something was good back then.

989
00:59:34,777 --> 00:59:39,897
And grandparents tell these things
to today's grandchildren.

990
00:59:40,836 --> 00:59:44,677
Because kids today listen to grandparents,
they're raised by them, not their parents.

991
00:59:44,717 --> 00:59:51,317
The parents are at work, and young people
today kept hearing their grandparents say

992
00:59:51,358 --> 00:59:53,358
that things were better under Ceaușescu.

993
00:59:54,777 --> 00:59:58,457
My parents don't tell my daughter that,
they went through what you did in Brașov.

994
00:59:58,717 --> 01:00:03,597
Yes, they went through other— The Communists
have hated Brașov ever since then.

995
01:00:03,697 --> 01:00:04,377
They still hate it today.

996
01:00:04,657 --> 01:00:06,657
Yes, and the people of Brașov hated communism.

997
01:00:07,097 --> 01:00:12,217
As proven by the 1987 uprising—
When I nearly died of peritonitis.

998
01:00:13,097 --> 01:00:13,237
Yes.

999
01:00:13,777 --> 01:00:14,897
I told the story of my appendicitis.

1000
01:00:15,297 --> 01:00:15,497
Exactly.

1001
01:00:16,438 --> 01:00:16,738
So...

1002
01:00:19,317 --> 01:00:20,117
there are the passéists.

1003
01:00:20,358 --> 01:00:24,738
Second are the progressives, those
who look to the future, who want a

1004
01:00:24,777 --> 01:00:29,157
better, more beautiful, cleaner, more
ethical, better-performing Romania.

1005
01:00:29,637 --> 01:00:33,836
And to get off the bottom places in
Europe on every performance indicator.

1006
01:00:34,297 --> 01:00:36,077
That is, a competitive Romania.

1007
01:00:36,478 --> 01:00:37,597
We are not competitive.

1008
01:00:37,878 --> 01:00:39,077
What's the difference between these two?

1009
01:00:40,057 --> 01:00:42,577
Well, some look to the future
and others to the past.

1010
01:00:42,617 --> 01:00:43,918
How do they come to think that way?

1011
01:00:46,494 --> 01:00:47,474
What's the fundamental difference?

1012
01:00:47,494 --> 01:00:51,134
I showed this drawing on your show,
now maybe we have a new audience.

1013
01:00:51,534 --> 01:00:52,334
Did you laminate it?

1014
01:00:52,693 --> 01:00:52,954
Yes.

1015
01:00:52,974 --> 01:00:53,874
I think that's really cool.

1016
01:00:53,914 --> 01:00:55,234
So I can carry it around.

1017
01:00:55,693 --> 01:00:56,873
Can you see it on camera?

1018
01:00:56,914 --> 01:00:57,614
It's very clearly visible.

1019
01:00:57,634 --> 01:00:57,914
Good.

1020
01:00:58,454 --> 01:01:03,934
It's Jastrow's famous drawing,
also called the

1021
01:01:03,994 --> 01:01:08,054
duck-rabbit drawing, which shows that
looking at the same reality, we see

1022
01:01:08,153 --> 01:01:09,873
things— we can see different things.

1023
01:01:10,594 --> 01:01:15,874
Whoever reads from
left to right will see a rabbit.

1024
01:01:16,414 --> 01:01:20,554
Whoever reads from
right to left, a duck.

1025
01:01:22,034 --> 01:01:25,733
And you know there are cultures
where people read right to left.

1026
01:01:25,793 --> 01:01:29,933
Arabic is read
from right to left.

1027
01:01:31,494 --> 01:01:33,934
Now I don't know why I always
see the duck first.

1028
01:01:37,998 --> 01:01:41,237
Because you read left to right,
in European text.

1029
01:01:42,458 --> 01:01:42,798
Right.

1030
01:01:42,858 --> 01:01:48,597
If you'd grown up with Hebrew—
You'd have seen the rabbit.

1031
01:01:48,657 --> 01:01:49,418
Now I get it.

1032
01:01:49,998 --> 01:01:54,918
So depending on the education
we have, on the reading we've

1033
01:01:54,958 --> 01:01:59,258
done, on life experience, we see
two different things in reality.

1034
01:01:59,697 --> 01:02:03,377
Some see a duck, others a rabbit,
some see the past and retreat into that

1035
01:02:03,418 --> 01:02:09,657
past to find some stable
ground in this world

1036
01:02:10,217 --> 01:02:15,917
without criteria and without—
Certainties. ...without certainties.

1037
01:02:16,358 --> 01:02:18,178
The others see the solution in the future.

1038
01:02:18,637 --> 01:02:23,777
Let's fix our democracy—it's not good,
it's fake, a mess, full of holes.

1039
01:02:24,717 --> 01:02:25,498
Sounds familiar.

1040
01:02:26,097 --> 01:02:31,657
So you have to fix it, but
going forward, in a better

1041
01:02:31,697 --> 01:02:33,617
Romania that will surpass that one.

1042
01:02:34,117 --> 01:02:35,898
And there's a third solution:

1043
01:02:38,389 --> 01:02:43,690
Stay put, stay put, stay put
and let the basil grow, stand still, don't

1044
01:02:43,789 --> 01:02:46,130
change anything, because we're fine.

1045
01:02:46,730 --> 01:02:47,469
We're doing fine.

1046
01:02:47,829 --> 01:02:52,989
They're the local barons, they're
the employees in

1047
01:02:53,929 --> 01:02:56,588
public administration.

1048
01:02:56,630 --> 01:03:01,009
So all these state employees doing well,
who don't do much but live well.

1049
01:03:01,429 --> 01:03:04,509
They have bonuses, they have special pensions.

1050
01:03:04,730 --> 01:03:05,150
They have it like that.

1051
01:03:05,789 --> 01:03:14,269
Former Securitate officers,
former army

1052
01:03:14,349 --> 01:03:16,449
officers with special pensions.

1053
01:03:16,829 --> 01:03:20,949
None of them want change, because
they don't know whether with change

1054
01:03:20,989 --> 01:03:25,088
they might lose their privileges and
have to change their way of life.

1055
01:03:25,528 --> 01:03:33,028
These people will cling tooth and nail
to the current system.

1056
01:03:33,548 --> 01:03:39,528
These people hate USR, they hate
Bolojan, because they've seen he

1057
01:03:39,568 --> 01:03:41,489
actually took his job seriously.

1058
01:03:41,769 --> 01:03:42,309
More or less.

1059
01:03:42,349 --> 01:03:43,327
That he wants change.

1060
01:03:43,650 --> 01:03:48,910
Well, just as he was about to move to
the big plan, they sidelined him.

1061
01:03:48,989 --> 01:03:50,789
Well, he could've tried
something in the first month.

1062
01:03:50,789 --> 01:03:52,130
We've had this discussion, Professor.

1063
01:03:52,250 --> 01:03:52,630
He couldn't.

1064
01:03:52,690 --> 01:03:53,450
Oh yes, he could.

1065
01:03:53,650 --> 01:03:55,130
He'd have had to go in— He'd
have been out in the first month.

1066
01:03:55,429 --> 01:03:57,108
Kimberly came later, Professor.

1067
01:03:57,170 --> 01:03:57,528
No, no.

1068
01:03:57,568 --> 01:03:58,730
He had to change laws.

1069
01:03:59,048 --> 01:03:59,150
Yes.

1070
01:03:59,670 --> 01:04:02,088
Well, with these guys, with PSD dominant.

1071
01:04:02,130 --> 01:04:03,509
At least show us he's trying.

1072
01:04:03,710 --> 01:04:05,909
I have the same argument
about Bolojan as about Nicușor.

1073
01:04:06,289 --> 01:04:08,949
They all complain that
there are more urgent things.

1074
01:04:09,028 --> 01:04:10,250
There's always something more urgent.

1075
01:04:11,009 --> 01:04:12,588
"Forget about Pahonțu,
we'll talk about it later.

1076
01:04:12,608 --> 01:04:16,190
I've assessed it, it's fine, we have
more important things, more..."

1077
01:04:16,230 --> 01:04:17,210
There's always something more urgent.

1078
01:04:17,230 --> 01:04:18,170
That's a false argument.

1079
01:04:18,230 --> 01:04:18,650
Agreed.

1080
01:04:19,170 --> 01:04:23,108
Just like the stability argument,
just like the unity argument.

1081
01:04:23,710 --> 01:04:24,329
Let's be clear.

1082
01:04:24,750 --> 01:04:27,650
When I hear that thing about stability
and unity— Exactly.

1083
01:04:28,170 --> 01:04:30,028
Well, unity in bad things—
Sweeping it under the rug.

1084
01:04:30,088 --> 01:04:32,130
Should we be united in misery?

1085
01:04:32,588 --> 01:04:33,190
I don't get it.

1086
01:04:33,528 --> 01:04:35,650
Stability in last place in Europe?

1087
01:04:36,190 --> 01:04:36,950
I've said this before.

1088
01:04:37,230 --> 01:04:40,509
Bolojan doesn't talk like
a liberal prime minister.

1089
01:04:40,568 --> 01:04:44,588
I haven't once heard him talk about
Romanian entrepreneurs or the middle class.

1090
01:04:44,670 --> 01:04:50,809
Because when the cart's in the ditch
and the horses are stuck in the fence,

1091
01:04:50,849 --> 01:04:52,150
you can't— Well, and he's
got us pushing it.

1092
01:04:52,469 --> 01:04:52,629
No.

1093
01:04:52,670 --> 01:04:54,869
With the state employees in it.
Yes, it has to be pulled out.

1094
01:04:54,909 --> 01:04:55,769
And how do you see me?

1095
01:04:56,048 --> 01:04:59,190
Liberal or— Well, then he should throw
some state employees off the cart.

1096
01:04:59,409 --> 01:05:00,909
So we pull the cart out,
them included?

1097
01:05:00,949 --> 01:05:06,889
How can you even think of it when they're
protected by the civil servants' law, they're

1098
01:05:06,929 --> 01:05:11,690
protected by the public pay law, they're
protected by... Laws had to be changed.

1099
01:05:11,750 --> 01:05:12,130
It would take time.

1100
01:05:12,909 --> 01:05:16,670
Whereas we had the Economic
Council of the European

1101
01:05:16,730 --> 01:05:21,108
Union waiting, and the assessment—
It'll be like that every time. The rating.

1102
01:05:21,329 --> 01:05:23,250
Like that— No, there really
was pressure back then.

1103
01:05:23,369 --> 01:05:27,230
I don't know, I think— He had to do
what he could in the time available.

1104
01:05:28,630 --> 01:05:33,150
When do you think we'll ever
have a liberal prime minister in

1105
01:05:33,210 --> 01:05:34,690
this country again, from now on, ever?

1106
01:05:38,269 --> 01:05:42,110
It depends on the result
of the early elections.

1107
01:05:42,409 --> 01:05:43,649
And I don't mean PNL here.

1108
01:05:44,010 --> 01:05:45,070
I mean— Liberal.

1109
01:05:45,289 --> 01:05:46,369
A liberal orientation.

1110
01:05:46,449 --> 01:05:47,730
Yes, a liberal orientation.

1111
01:05:48,389 --> 01:05:55,629
Because even in PNL there are those 25
conservatives, as they were counted recently,

1112
01:05:55,969 --> 01:05:58,129
who want to keep the status quo.

1113
01:05:58,889 --> 01:06:01,670
Let things change, but keep—
how did that go, the essentials.

1114
01:06:01,889 --> 01:06:02,109
Yes.

1115
01:06:02,150 --> 01:06:07,070
Let it stay— So they're PNL members,
but they don't care about change.

1116
01:06:07,710 --> 01:06:10,409
Whereas that's what liberalism means.

1117
01:06:10,469 --> 01:06:16,409
It also means a mind, an open way
of thinking that takes reality into account

1118
01:06:16,969 --> 01:06:21,869
and shapes itself according to
the internal changes in reality.

1119
01:06:24,030 --> 01:06:27,769
Have you ever noticed, when you pass
the Arc de Triomphe, that there's— there

1120
01:06:27,769 --> 01:06:32,170
used to be another name on that
building, and now there's a party

1121
01:06:32,190 --> 01:06:35,490
called the Humanist Social Liberal Party.

1122
01:06:36,389 --> 01:06:38,230
The one on the right as you head downtown.

1123
01:06:38,329 --> 01:06:38,429
Yes.

1124
01:06:38,570 --> 01:06:39,170
Yes, I've seen it.

1125
01:06:40,070 --> 01:06:41,170
How does that sound to you?

1126
01:06:41,230 --> 01:06:42,789
Humanist and social and liberal.

1127
01:06:43,550 --> 01:06:49,130
There's a strand of social liberalism
that says: look, freedom,

1128
01:06:50,130 --> 01:06:56,210
free enterprise, entrepreneurship, but
with state regulation when it comes

1129
01:06:56,250 --> 01:06:58,809
to social policies.

1130
01:06:58,909 --> 01:06:59,250
Oh, OK.

1131
01:06:59,590 --> 01:07:01,530
So there's a tension there.

1132
01:07:01,670 --> 01:07:05,110
Meaning the state shouldn't
intervene in economic life,

1133
01:07:05,230 --> 01:07:06,949
but should act as a traffic cop.

1134
01:07:07,750 --> 01:07:09,949
A traffic cop doesn't make
you go to Brașov.

1135
01:07:10,090 --> 01:07:14,590
You can go to Ploiești, wherever
you want, to Constanța, to the seaside, but

1136
01:07:14,630 --> 01:07:16,570
you obey the traffic rules.

1137
01:07:16,969 --> 01:07:20,109
So in principle it'd make sense,
because— The Competition Council,

1138
01:07:20,150 --> 01:07:22,050
for instance, is a state body.

1139
01:07:22,389 --> 01:07:22,489
Yes.

1140
01:07:23,170 --> 01:07:27,230
All the laws
that regulate business.

1141
01:07:27,750 --> 01:07:32,369
So it's liberalism, but under
conditions defined by the state.

1142
01:07:33,429 --> 01:07:36,869
We've had this discussion here before,
touched on it several times, but I dare you

1143
01:07:36,909 --> 01:07:41,289
to touch on it a bit more, because I see
another source of tension in this society.

1144
01:07:41,969 --> 01:07:45,869
You told us about the passéists,
about those who want— the progressives

1145
01:07:45,909 --> 01:07:47,049
and those who want us to stay put, right?

1146
01:07:49,893 --> 01:07:50,113
Mhm.

1147
01:07:50,574 --> 01:07:54,253
They overlap across all these
layers, but I think there's

1148
01:07:54,273 --> 01:07:55,893
another tension linked to change.

1149
01:07:56,713 --> 01:08:00,452
The changes. From our very first talks
you've told me about the gap

1150
01:08:00,472 --> 01:08:04,253
between generations, which used to be small.

1151
01:08:05,074 --> 01:08:07,874
Kids could talk to their parents
and understand each other.

1152
01:08:08,472 --> 01:08:09,074
Not anymore.

1153
01:08:10,134 --> 01:08:13,894
And from this gap here,
it went this way, then

1154
01:08:13,972 --> 01:08:15,134
that way, and then that way.

1155
01:08:15,432 --> 01:08:17,333
And this one here and those
over there no longer— they neither

1156
01:08:17,373 --> 01:08:18,293
see nor hear each other anymore.

1157
01:08:18,912 --> 01:08:21,753
We were talking a bit earlier,
for example, about that Gen Z

1158
01:08:21,873 --> 01:08:23,813
stare I told you about.

1159
01:08:24,054 --> 01:08:25,953
Tell our viewers about it too.

1160
01:08:26,094 --> 01:08:30,854
When a kid looks at you, a teenager,
or not, it can be someone in their twenties.

1161
01:08:31,094 --> 01:08:32,773
It can even be a civil servant.

1162
01:08:33,493 --> 01:08:33,673
Yes.

1163
01:08:34,554 --> 01:08:38,033
It can be someone at a counter,
at an airline

1164
01:08:38,073 --> 01:08:43,153
counter, someone from a big company
you're dealing with.

1165
01:08:43,514 --> 01:08:44,314
It happened to me yesterday.

1166
01:08:44,354 --> 01:08:48,273
I gave you some examples: I was
on a trip and I ran into

1167
01:08:48,314 --> 01:08:55,242
this attitude from the person you're
literally face to face with. And they look

1168
01:08:55,282 --> 01:08:57,622
right through you. The corporation is at fault.

1169
01:08:58,502 --> 01:08:58,702
Yes.

1170
01:08:58,962 --> 01:09:02,682
It delayed the flight until—
Let's say something happened

1171
01:09:02,782 --> 01:09:03,962
that's not right.

1172
01:09:04,582 --> 01:09:11,261
His only goal in his dealings
with you is for you to disappear

1173
01:09:11,301 --> 01:09:18,521
from his sight one way or another, with
minimum effort and minimum impact

1174
01:09:18,622 --> 01:09:20,582
on his schedule and his daily stress.

1175
01:09:20,720 --> 01:09:21,541
I'm being very serious.

1176
01:09:21,841 --> 01:09:23,682
Yes, I know, but you tell it so well.

1177
01:09:23,822 --> 01:09:25,862
That's why I like what I'm hearing.

1178
01:09:26,681 --> 01:09:31,742
We all tend toward minimum effort,
but these people, in this new

1179
01:09:31,801 --> 01:09:35,301
generation doing this Gen Z
stare, they look right through you.

1180
01:09:35,862 --> 01:09:38,061
My colleagues say— Alina
calls it a poker face.

1181
01:09:38,601 --> 01:09:40,322
A poker face at least has an endgame.

1182
01:09:41,720 --> 01:09:43,921
They give you a poker face,
bluff you and take your money.

1183
01:09:44,702 --> 01:09:45,622
No, this one wants nothing from you.

1184
01:09:45,641 --> 01:09:46,260
They just want you gone.

1185
01:09:46,662 --> 01:09:49,822
They just want to get rid of you,
even if their job is to take care

1186
01:09:49,902 --> 01:09:53,322
of a sick old man who was
left at the airport at 1 a.m.

1187
01:09:53,702 --> 01:09:55,442
I'm talking about yet another airline.

1188
01:09:56,042 --> 01:09:57,382
I don't want to go into too much detail.

1189
01:09:57,402 --> 01:10:00,021
I was left stranded on the plane,
at the airport last night.

1190
01:10:00,320 --> 01:10:01,841
That's why I was late
for today's recording.

1191
01:10:02,141 --> 01:10:02,981
You'll still get it on time.

1192
01:10:04,341 --> 01:10:05,981
Courtesy of the famous Wizz Air.

1193
01:10:06,261 --> 01:10:07,282
It happened to me with Ryanair too.

1194
01:10:08,761 --> 01:10:12,701
These companies, since we were talking
about this capitalism, managed to

1195
01:10:12,921 --> 01:10:16,721
push the classic scheduled airlines
off the market, beat them on price,

1196
01:10:17,601 --> 01:10:19,461
tricked us all into using them.

1197
01:10:19,782 --> 01:10:23,221
Those went bankrupt or pulled out
of most routes, and now

1198
01:10:23,242 --> 01:10:27,322
when you have a problem, you talk to
one person, and the person at the counter

1199
01:10:27,341 --> 01:10:31,461
told me, after I asked her:
OK, what assistance can you give me?

1200
01:10:31,481 --> 01:10:32,481
It's 1 a.m.

1201
01:10:33,021 --> 01:10:34,681
The hotels near the airport are full.

1202
01:10:35,082 --> 01:10:35,542
What do we do?

1203
01:10:36,162 --> 01:10:37,462
And she says: "Well, you'll manage."

1204
01:10:38,902 --> 01:10:40,681
But you represent the company.

1205
01:10:40,761 --> 01:10:41,501
What's the assistance?

1206
01:10:42,641 --> 01:10:43,242
"We don't have any."

1207
01:10:44,881 --> 01:10:45,701
You'll get a phone call.

1208
01:10:46,082 --> 01:10:47,462
You felt they didn't respect you.

1209
01:10:48,242 --> 01:10:49,302
No, it's more than that, you know.

1210
01:10:49,322 --> 01:10:50,122
It's not about respect.

1211
01:10:50,162 --> 01:10:51,742
Wait, wait, I want to go further.

1212
01:10:52,021 --> 01:10:52,582
There's no respect there.

1213
01:10:53,061 --> 01:10:53,782
They don't respect you.

1214
01:10:54,381 --> 01:10:55,561
Do you know why they don't respect you?

1215
01:10:56,082 --> 01:10:58,422
Because you fly with this airline.

1216
01:10:59,582 --> 01:11:00,802
Well, he was working for them.

1217
01:11:01,301 --> 01:11:04,862
Yes, but the airline knows
it caters to the working classes.

1218
01:11:05,721 --> 01:11:09,822
It took the place— But there's no
alternative, I don't know if you see.

1219
01:11:09,881 --> 01:11:10,361
I believe you.

1220
01:11:11,202 --> 01:11:11,521
I know.

1221
01:11:11,622 --> 01:11:12,922
Know how much I paid for the next flight?

1222
01:11:12,962 --> 01:11:17,562
Can you afford to pay— I paid
340 euros for the next flight

1223
01:11:17,601 --> 01:11:20,301
for a middle seat, just to get home today.

1224
01:11:20,822 --> 01:11:25,462
Because the alternative was being
rerouted within the next 14 days, at some

1225
01:11:25,481 --> 01:11:27,362
point, whenever the airline felt like it.

1226
01:11:27,481 --> 01:11:30,282
Not— According to the company's
internal regulations.

1227
01:11:30,662 --> 01:11:31,402
Yes, yes.

1228
01:11:31,681 --> 01:11:33,761
As you were saying
in a previous episode—

1229
01:11:33,801 --> 01:11:35,101
Institutional autism, yes.

1230
01:11:35,421 --> 01:11:39,741
They— There's the law, and there's,
what do they call it, corporate policy,

1231
01:11:39,761 --> 01:11:41,282
or as they put it, "our organizational culture."

1232
01:11:41,322 --> 01:11:41,981
Yes, yes, yes.

1233
01:11:43,082 --> 01:11:44,942
Well, I want to tell you this.

1234
01:11:45,582 --> 01:11:50,162
These airlines actually
adapted to the new audiences.

1235
01:11:51,082 --> 01:11:58,622
Until around the '90s, in Europe
at least, the people flying were

1236
01:11:58,702 --> 01:12:00,622
the upper class— Because it was expensive.

1237
01:12:01,002 --> 01:12:04,602
Yes, and the middle class.

1238
01:12:04,681 --> 01:12:04,862
Uh-huh.

1239
01:12:05,181 --> 01:12:06,541
They were well-off people.

1240
01:12:06,801 --> 01:12:06,862
Yes.

1241
01:12:07,141 --> 01:12:11,921
When migration intensified,
so after the opening of the European

1242
01:12:11,942 --> 01:12:18,362
Union, the working classes
started flying.

1243
01:12:19,341 --> 01:12:24,261
And then the airlines said: "Wait, we'll
invent, reinvent ourselves as

1244
01:12:24,322 --> 01:12:27,962
low-cost companies for this new audience."

1245
01:12:28,702 --> 01:12:34,902
That's why Air France
and Lufthansa and

1246
01:12:34,962 --> 01:12:36,482
the rest went bust— Alitalia, all of them.

1247
01:12:36,521 --> 01:12:37,181
Alitalia.

1248
01:12:37,662 --> 01:12:41,122
Air France and Lufthansa
merged to save themselves.

1249
01:12:41,442 --> 01:12:42,421
No, Air France-KLM.

1250
01:12:43,082 --> 01:12:43,902
KLM, right.

1251
01:12:43,942 --> 01:12:46,942
Air France-KLM, Lufthansa— The Dutch…
and with Swiss, there are now

1252
01:12:46,942 --> 01:12:48,042
several alliances like that.

1253
01:12:48,421 --> 01:12:54,721
To save themselves. Because, you see,
the way I felt under

1254
01:12:54,742 --> 01:13:00,622
Ceaușescu, when I was standing
in line, a huge line, to get

1255
01:13:00,942 --> 01:13:06,942
two loaves of bread, those people mocked us
because to them we were nobodies.

1256
01:13:07,681 --> 01:13:07,841
Yes.

1257
01:13:08,162 --> 01:13:12,502
Because the policeman— Few people
understand this— The neighborhood

1258
01:13:12,542 --> 01:13:15,481
policeman— …that contempt…
and the bosses got theirs out the back.

1259
01:13:15,742 --> 01:13:15,842
Yes.

1260
01:13:16,542 --> 01:13:20,002
Others went there, meaning
the representatives of the system.

1261
01:13:20,402 --> 01:13:24,381
We, standing in line, were
the slaves of the system— Yes… and

1262
01:13:24,421 --> 01:13:25,761
she treated us like slaves.

1263
01:13:26,362 --> 01:13:28,462
Because she knew who we were,
or we wouldn't have been there.

1264
01:13:28,721 --> 01:13:28,822
Yes.

1265
01:13:29,662 --> 01:13:30,862
We were people without power.

1266
01:13:31,282 --> 01:13:36,342
Yes, soft power, as they say
in political science jargon.

1267
01:13:36,902 --> 01:13:42,377
So— That's what happened to you.
There's one more nuance there.

1268
01:13:43,037 --> 01:13:44,257
Technology changed as well.

1269
01:13:44,318 --> 01:13:47,538
Planes became more efficient,
a new kind of management appeared,

1270
01:13:47,556 --> 01:13:51,557
and a different business model that
made tickets so cheap

1271
01:13:51,597 --> 01:13:53,057
they ended up competing with coaches.

1272
01:13:54,457 --> 01:13:56,317
Because that migration
used to happen by coach.

1273
01:13:56,377 --> 01:13:56,478
Yes.

1274
01:13:57,297 --> 01:14:00,377
And I sat there thinking how good
the conditions on the coach were

1275
01:14:00,417 --> 01:14:02,177
compared to this bus with wings.

1276
01:14:04,037 --> 01:14:04,737
That's right.

1277
01:14:05,398 --> 01:14:12,557
That's how I felt once coming back
from— from Brussels, I think it was.

1278
01:14:13,497 --> 01:14:15,977
We waited and waited and waited.

1279
01:14:15,997 --> 01:14:18,537
The Bucharest flight was delayed like that too.

1280
01:14:19,078 --> 01:14:23,598
In the end they let us through
at the gate there.

1281
01:14:24,118 --> 01:14:30,298
We had to go down one or two
levels to get to

1282
01:14:30,978 --> 01:14:33,297
the actual exit from the terminal.

1283
01:14:36,010 --> 01:14:42,430
And on the stairs they stop us and say:
"We have to wait for the actual exit

1284
01:14:42,489 --> 01:14:44,010
to open, it hasn't opened yet."

1285
01:14:44,569 --> 01:14:49,789
And we stood there for some 40 minutes
in the heat, in a— In a

1286
01:14:49,890 --> 01:14:51,329
hall that looked like a stable.

1287
01:14:52,390 --> 01:14:53,990
It wasn't even a hall, it was stairs.

1288
01:14:55,289 --> 01:14:56,010
You understand?

1289
01:14:56,050 --> 01:15:00,369
We were stuck between two landings.

1290
01:15:00,649 --> 01:15:01,509
Don't you find it strange

1291
01:15:03,609 --> 01:15:09,989
that in the end we always end up with
absolutely ugly buildings

1292
01:15:10,949 --> 01:15:14,729
that serve nothing but a bare minimum
purpose: keeping the rain off you?

1293
01:15:15,890 --> 01:15:16,170
That's it.

1294
01:15:17,050 --> 01:15:18,750
I was looking at exactly
what you're describing, in

1295
01:15:18,810 --> 01:15:22,710
Bologna. I went to the plane,
and there's no jet bridge.

1296
01:15:23,170 --> 01:15:27,810
You take a bus to get to the plane,
and you go down a metal staircase,

1297
01:15:27,890 --> 01:15:31,269
through a metal structure that
looks exactly like a factory floor.

1298
01:15:31,689 --> 01:15:35,268
I've seen nicer factory halls
than what airports have lately.

1299
01:15:36,289 --> 01:15:39,470
Well, in architecture this was
called the International Style.

1300
01:15:40,168 --> 01:15:41,349
Metal and glass.

1301
01:15:42,329 --> 01:15:44,210
It's univer— speaking of universalism.

1302
01:15:44,649 --> 01:15:46,809
No, it's not even— there's
no architecture there,

1303
01:15:46,829 --> 01:15:47,890
Professor, there's only function.

1304
01:15:47,909 --> 01:15:48,649
Well, I know, I know.

1305
01:15:49,109 --> 01:15:50,268
That's functionalism.

1306
01:15:50,649 --> 01:15:52,809
If it's function, it serves us all.

1307
01:15:53,289 --> 01:15:56,039
But what disappears is that...

1308
01:15:58,761 --> 01:16:05,021
That chic of national character,
of particularism, you don't see anymore.

1309
01:16:05,742 --> 01:16:09,742
Henri Coandă Airport, right?

1310
01:16:10,561 --> 01:16:21,561
It has that building from '68-'69,
the Arrivals one, that

1311
01:16:24,181 --> 01:16:25,981
shows Romanian architecture.

1312
01:16:26,381 --> 01:16:26,581
Uh-huh.

1313
01:16:26,761 --> 01:16:30,301
Because it's a cultural allusion to that.

1314
01:16:30,942 --> 01:16:32,122
The thatched houses?

1315
01:16:32,761 --> 01:16:34,421
Yes, something like that.

1316
01:16:34,721 --> 01:16:34,942
OK.

1317
01:16:35,261 --> 01:16:38,141
I think Creangă was the designer,
I don't remember, one

1318
01:16:38,181 --> 01:16:39,341
of our great architects.

1319
01:16:39,521 --> 01:16:39,721
OK.

1320
01:16:40,301 --> 01:16:45,442
But this Departures terminal,

1321
01:16:47,761 --> 01:16:48,421
have you seen it?

1322
01:16:48,561 --> 01:16:52,162
It's a huge hall of metal and glass.

1323
01:16:52,462 --> 01:16:52,542
Yes.

1324
01:16:53,862 --> 01:16:55,122
That's the International Style.

1325
01:16:55,681 --> 01:16:58,621
If you like, for those of you who
want to recognize the International

1326
01:16:58,881 --> 01:17:03,442
Style, look at the
United Nations headquarters.

1327
01:17:03,981 --> 01:17:04,061
Yes.

1328
01:17:04,822 --> 01:17:07,882
It's a block of glass and concrete.

1329
01:17:08,442 --> 01:17:09,381
I'd take it further.

1330
01:17:11,261 --> 01:17:13,181
So I see this tension between

1331
01:17:16,326 --> 01:17:21,605
people who feel left behind, who
used to come back from abroad,

1332
01:17:22,105 --> 01:17:26,305
even on low-cost flights or by coach,
with 1,000 euros in their pocket,

1333
01:17:28,729 --> 01:17:33,069
and back in their village people gathered,
there was a good vibe around them,

1334
01:17:33,109 --> 01:17:35,089
a party, and they were respected.

1335
01:17:35,649 --> 01:17:40,349
They had respect, more than
respect, they had authority.

1336
01:17:40,430 --> 01:17:42,789
They came from somewhere
far away with some extra money.

1337
01:17:43,149 --> 01:17:43,349
Yes.

1338
01:17:44,090 --> 01:17:47,829
And now life here has pretty much
caught up with life there, and they

1339
01:17:47,869 --> 01:17:49,670
are treated like anyone else.

1340
01:17:50,229 --> 01:17:54,529
What's more, along comes some
brainiac politician who tells them

1341
01:17:54,550 --> 01:17:57,110
they don't matter, "oh, the diaspora."

1342
01:17:57,289 --> 01:18:00,829
"What's with these diaspora people
coming to tell us what to do?"

1343
01:18:00,889 --> 01:18:04,470
And sometimes they feel invalidated.

1344
01:18:05,329 --> 01:18:08,430
Then there are those who stayed behind
and wait for that hundred euros

1345
01:18:08,489 --> 01:18:12,869
sent home, or the money put on Revolut
from the West, who are no longer happy

1346
01:18:12,869 --> 01:18:17,750
with just a kilo of flour and two
of oil from the mayor, and aren't

1347
01:18:17,770 --> 01:18:19,150
as easy to control anymore.

1348
01:18:20,409 --> 01:18:22,909
Then there's all this softening up
of the ground that

1349
01:18:22,949 --> 01:18:24,809
we talked about, on social media.

1350
01:18:26,390 --> 01:18:30,130
We have the city people who look
outward, and then we have those

1351
01:18:30,149 --> 01:18:37,129
from this rural world, physically but
also mentally, who feel left behind.

1352
01:18:37,850 --> 01:18:38,390
Am I seeing this right?

1353
01:18:39,510 --> 01:18:41,289
Yes, and there's another dimension.

1354
01:18:41,970 --> 01:18:42,890
You see it very clearly.

1355
01:18:44,289 --> 01:18:49,189
I've seen it in people who left
long ago, before the Revolution,

1356
01:18:49,789 --> 01:18:57,229
who became citizens of foreign
countries, and after the Revolution and after

1357
01:18:57,270 --> 01:18:58,930
our accession to the European Union.

1358
01:18:59,869 --> 01:19:04,210
You said a moment ago
that market standards have

1359
01:19:04,270 --> 01:19:12,510
evened out, and they ask me: what do you think
I would have become if I hadn't left?

1360
01:19:14,869 --> 01:19:19,529
They try to figure out whether
all their effort to integrate, to

1361
01:19:19,590 --> 01:19:25,050
resocialize, to learn the language,
to change their habits, all

1362
01:19:25,569 --> 01:19:30,509
that ordeal, which can't help but
leave trauma, whether it

1363
01:19:30,529 --> 01:19:37,509
was all for nothing, whether they were
wrong, whether history fooled them.

1364
01:19:39,248 --> 01:19:45,710
They try to picture what they
might have been, and I try to tell them

1365
01:19:46,649 --> 01:19:48,269
as honestly as I can what I think.

1366
01:19:48,909 --> 01:19:52,789
I think some of them could have
made it in Romania.

1367
01:19:54,250 --> 01:19:54,670
Probably.

1368
01:19:54,689 --> 01:19:56,029
Yes, they could have made it.

1369
01:19:56,329 --> 01:19:57,430
But they couldn't have known back then.

1370
01:19:57,569 --> 01:19:58,429
No, they couldn't have known.

1371
01:19:58,449 --> 01:20:03,889
They stood in those lines at— At the
German Embassy, in Piața Dorobanți.

1372
01:20:04,529 --> 01:20:07,189
Or over there— They slept overnight…
at Sala Palatului, again,

1373
01:20:07,210 --> 01:20:08,390
for work permits.

1374
01:20:08,869 --> 01:20:11,869
Yes, the lines at Băneasa
for low-cost flights and so on.

1375
01:20:12,649 --> 01:20:18,433
I had a scene like that with someone
at the airport, a Roma lady who

1376
01:20:18,453 --> 01:20:22,253
was on the phone with someone, pretty
loudly, so I couldn't help hearing her,

1377
01:20:22,274 --> 01:20:24,374
saying: "No, back home among the Roma it's nice.

1378
01:20:24,453 --> 01:20:28,413
When I go home, everyone asks me
how I'm doing, invites me for coffee.

1379
01:20:28,474 --> 01:20:33,833
Come, here, look what I made, they bring
meat out of the lard crock for us to eat.

1380
01:20:34,613 --> 01:20:35,473
We talk to each other.

1381
01:20:35,774 --> 01:20:40,474
With the Romanians, when I go"—I think
it was a mixed family—she says, "when

1382
01:20:40,493 --> 01:20:44,373
I go over to the Romanians, nobody talks,
nobody pays me any attention.

1383
01:20:44,774 --> 01:20:45,994
These people don't really talk.

1384
01:20:46,314 --> 01:20:49,274
Among the Roma, everyone talks
to everyone, back home—"

1385
01:20:49,894 --> 01:20:52,734
So there are a lot of layers
and a lot of differences.

1386
01:20:53,234 --> 01:20:56,814
She says: "I don't know what to do now,
my clothes are in some suitcases,

1387
01:20:56,814 --> 01:21:00,894
I have clothes in Italy, clothes here. I don't
even know what clothes I have, they're

1388
01:21:00,974 --> 01:21:02,373
all over the suitcases, in three places."

1389
01:21:03,453 --> 01:21:04,733
So there's another layer.

1390
01:21:05,434 --> 01:21:08,434
You have clothes in Italy, clothes in the
Roma quarter, and clothes at the Romanians'.

1391
01:21:09,753 --> 01:21:10,413
Interesting.

1392
01:21:11,993 --> 01:21:16,273
These are people who feel somehow
unmoored, uprooted

1393
01:21:16,314 --> 01:21:17,134
in this new world.

1394
01:21:17,173 --> 01:21:19,814
And what you heard,
that comparison

1395
01:21:19,873 --> 01:21:21,153
she was making, that's what she did.

1396
01:21:21,194 --> 01:21:26,314
Not quite a comparative analysis,
but a comparative description

1397
01:21:27,453 --> 01:21:29,233
of two types of civilization.

1398
01:21:30,113 --> 01:21:32,893
She was actually
praising community.

1399
01:21:33,173 --> 01:21:34,474
In a way— She was talking more— Exactly.

1400
01:21:34,913 --> 01:21:39,113
In a community, people ask each other
what you've been up to, how it went.

1401
01:21:39,173 --> 01:21:40,134
Come over for a coffee.

1402
01:21:40,354 --> 01:21:40,494
Yes.

1403
01:21:40,634 --> 01:21:41,453
Let's have a chat.

1404
01:21:43,333 --> 01:21:48,594
With the Romanians she feels that individualism,
everyone on their own, and maybe,

1405
01:21:48,634 --> 01:21:51,793
if I talk to my son and my daughter,
because often I don't

1406
01:21:51,833 --> 01:21:53,913
even talk to them, I don't have time for them.

1407
01:21:54,573 --> 01:22:00,113
So she really sensed different
cultural models, different patterns.

1408
01:22:01,413 --> 01:22:04,733
If we understand this, how did these
people from the countryside come together

1409
01:22:04,774 --> 01:22:11,534
in this movement we call sovereigntist,
which is actually nationalist?

1410
01:22:12,734 --> 01:22:16,634
Yes, it's nationalism christened,
rechristened as sovereigntism.

1411
01:22:19,613 --> 01:22:24,313
If they don't talk to each other, how do they
end up talking in this form of organization?

1412
01:22:24,774 --> 01:22:26,954
Go on TikTok and you'll see.

1413
01:22:28,810 --> 01:22:33,930
TikTok is made by those smart
operators who want to make money.

1414
01:22:34,930 --> 01:22:42,310
It's made for people like these who
have no reading habit, no patience

1415
01:22:42,369 --> 01:22:45,010
to read a whole page, but

1416
01:22:47,409 --> 01:22:54,609
through images, few words
and short clips, they can be influenced.

1417
01:22:56,390 --> 01:23:02,010
And they do get influenced because, Mr.
Buhnici, I recently discovered a book.

1418
01:23:02,449 --> 01:23:04,809
Unfortunately, a bit late.

1419
01:23:04,949 --> 01:23:09,250
It came out in Romanian in 2004.

1420
01:23:09,590 --> 01:23:13,390
2024, sorry, two years ago,
at Publica publishing house.

1421
01:23:14,149 --> 01:23:23,269
A book by an Indian man who was
vice president of the World Bank.

1422
